  <data key="d8">reduced NADP</data>
</node>
<node id="NRF1">
  <data key="d1">NRF1</data>
  <data key="d2">Nuclear respiratory factor 1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Transcription factor coactivated by PGC-1α that induces nuclear-encoded respiratory chain subunits and TFAM, supplying the missing mechanistic step between the YY1–PGC-1α complex and mitochondrial biogenesis.</data>
  <data key="d7">NRF1</data>
  <data key="d8">NRF-1</data>
</node>
<node id="PFKFB3">
  <data key="d1">PFKFB3</data>
  <data key="d2">PFKFB3</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Bifunctional kinase/phosphatase producing fructose-2,6-bisphosphate, the allosteric activator of PFK1; induced by HIF-1alpha downstream of mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d7">PFKFB3</data>
  <data key="d8">iPFK2</data>
</node>
<node id="PI3P">
  <data key="d1">PI3P</data>
  <data key="d2">Phosphatidylinositol 3-phosphate (PI3P)</data>
  <data key="d3">lipid</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Lipid product of the VPS34 (PIK3C3) complex I on the nascent phagophore; the signal that recruits PROPPIN/WIPI effectors and nucleates autophagosome formation.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">PtdIns3P</data>
</node>
<node id="PIK3C3_COMPLEX_I">
  <data key="d1">PIK3C3_COMPLEX_I</data>
  <data key="d2">Class III PI3K complex I</data>
  <data key="d3">complex</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Autophagy-specific VPS34-Beclin-1-ATG14L-VPS15 complex that produces PI(3)P at the nascent phagophore.</data>
  <data key="d7">PIK3C3, BECN1, ATG14, PIK3R4</data>
  <data key="d8">PI3KC3-C1</data>
</node>
<node id="PIK3C3_COMPLEX_II">
  <data key="d1">PIK3C3_COMPLEX_II</data>
  <data key="d2">PI3KC3 complex II (VPS34–Beclin-1–UVRAG)</data>
  <data key="d3">complex</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">UVRAG-containing class III PI3K complex, distinguished from ATG14L-containing complex I; acts in autophagosome maturation and endosomal trafficking rather than phagophore nucleation.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">VPS34 complex II</data>
</node>
<node id="PKM2">
  <data key="d1">PKM2</data>
  <data key="d2">PKM2</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">M2 splice isoform of pyruvate kinase favoured in proliferating cells; its low-activity state permits accumulation of glycolytic intermediates for biosynthesis.</data>
  <data key="d7">PKM</data>
  <data key="d8">pyruvate kinase M2</data>
</node>
<node id="PPARG">
  <data key="d1">PPARG</data>
  <data key="d2">PPARgamma</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Master adipogenic transcription factor; the PPARgamma programme is an adipose mTORC1-dependent output alongside adipogenesis.</data>
  <data key="d7">PPARG</data>
  <data key="d8">PPARg; peroxisome proliferator-activated receptor gamma</data>
</node>
<node id="PPARGC1A">
  <data key="d1">PPARGC1A</data>
  <data key="d2">PGC-1alpha</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Transcriptional coactivator of mitochondrial biogenesis and oxidative metabolism; its interaction with YY1 requires mTOR activity.</data>
  <data key="d7">PPARGC1A</data>
  <data key="d8">PGC-1a</data>
</node>
<node id="RIBOSE_5_PHOSPHATE">
  <data key="d1">RIBOSE_5_PHOSPHATE</data>
  <data key="d2">Ribose-5-phosphate</data>
  <data key="d3">metabolite</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Pentose phosphate pathway product supplying the ribose backbone for de novo purine and pyrimidine nucleotide synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">R5P</data>
</node>
<node id="SCD">
  <data key="d1">SCD</data>
  <data key="d2">Stearoyl-CoA desaturase 1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">SREBP1 target introducing the Δ9 double bond into saturated fatty acyl-CoAs, required for membrane phospholipid and triglyceride synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d7">SCD</data>
  <data key="d8">SCD1</data>
</node>
<node id="SIRT4">
  <data key="d1">SIRT4</data>
  <data key="d2">SIRT4</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Mitochondrial sirtuin that ADP-ribosylates and inhibits glutamate dehydrogenase; its expression is repressed downstream of mTORC1, de-repressing glutamine anaplerosis.</data>
  <data key="d7">SIRT4</data>
  <data key="d8">sirtuin 4</data>
</node>
<node id="SLC2A1">
  <data key="d1">SLC2A1</data>
  <data key="d2">GLUT1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Facilitative glucose transporter induced by HIF-1alpha, raising glucose uptake downstream of mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d7">SLC2A1</data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="SREBF1">
  <data key="d1">SREBF1</data>
  <data key="d2">SREBP1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Membrane-bound transcription factor whose proteolytic processing and nuclear accumulation are promoted by mTORC1; drives fatty-acid synthesis genes and G6PD.</data>
  <data key="d7">SREBF1</data>
  <data key="d8">SREBP-1c; SREBP1c</data>
</node>
<node id="SREBF2">
  <data key="d1">SREBF2</data>
  <data key="d2">SREBP2</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">SREBP isoform controlling the sterol biosynthetic gene programme (HMGCR, HMGCS1, LDLR); mTOR is required for its transcriptional output, partly through rapamycin-resistant mechanisms.</data>
  <data key="d7">SREBF2</data>
  <data key="d8">SREBP-2</data>
</node>
<node id="TFAM">
  <data key="d1">TFAM</data>
  <data key="d2">Mitochondrial transcription factor A</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Nuclear-encoded mtDNA packaging and transcription factor. Its translation is 4E-BP-sensitive, making it a concrete effector of the 4E-BP arm of mitochondrial biogenesis.</data>
  <data key="d7">TFAM</data>
  <data key="d8">mtTFA</data>
</node>
<node id="YWHA">
  <data key="d1">YWHA</data>
  <data key="d2">14-3-3</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Phosphoserine/threonine-binding adaptor family; binds AMPK-phosphorylated Raptor, S6K1-phosphorylated Rictor and Thr246-phosphorylated PRAS40, translating phosphorylation of core subunits into sequestration or inhibition.</data>
  <data key="d7">YWHAZ</data>
  <data key="d8">14-3-3 proteins</data>
</node>
<node id="YY1">
  <data key="d1">YY1</data>
  <data key="d2">YY1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Zinc-finger transcription factor that partners PGC-1alpha at mitochondrial gene promoters in an mTOR-dependent, rapamycin-sensitive manner.</data>
  <data key="d7">YY1</data>
  <data key="d8">Yin Yang 1</data>
</node>
<node id="ZFP36L1">
  <data key="d1">ZFP36L1</data>
  <data key="d2">ZFP36L1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">AU-rich-element RNA-binding protein that degrades cytokine transcripts; MK2 phosphorylation inactivates it, permitting SASP cytokine accumulation.</data>
  <data key="d7">ZFP36L1</data>
  <data key="d8">BRF1</data>
</node>
<node id="ATP1A1">
  <data key="d1">ATP1A1</data>
  <data key="d2">Na+/K+-ATPase (α1)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Basolateral sodium pump providing the driving force for transepithelial sodium reabsorption; stimulated downstream of SGK1.</data>
  <data key="d7">ATP1A1</data>
  <data key="d8">sodium-potassium ATPase</data>
</node>
<node id="BAD">
  <data key="d1">BAD</data>
  <data key="d2">BAD</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Pro-apoptotic BH3-only protein; phosphorylation at human Ser99 (mouse Ser136) promotes 14-3-3 binding and suppresses its apoptotic activity.</data>
  <data key="d7">BAD</data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="CDKN1B">
  <data key="d1">CDKN1B</data>
  <data key="d2">p27 (CDKN1B)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Cyclin-dependent kinase inhibitor; a FOXO1 transcriptional target. Elevated p27 accompanies nuclear FOXO1 accumulation in Rictor-null beta-cells and contributes to the reduced beta-cell mass seen on mTORC2 loss.</data>
  <data key="d7">CDKN1B</data>
  <data key="d8">p27Kip1</data>
</node>
<node id="CLCNK">
  <data key="d1">CLCNK</data>
  <data key="d2">CLC-K chloride channels</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Basolateral renal chloride channels (with barttin) required for transepithelial salt transport; positively regulated by SGK1.</data>
  <data key="d7">CLCNKB</data>
  <data key="d8">CLC-K1/K2; ClC-Kb</data>
</node>
<node id="FOXO1">
  <data key="d1">FOXO1</data>
  <data key="d2">FOXO1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Forkhead transcription factor; AKT phosphorylation (markedly enhanced by Ser473) causes 14-3-3 binding and nuclear exclusion, suppressing gluconeogenic and pro-apoptotic transcription.</data>
  <data key="d7">FOXO1</data>
  <data key="d8">FKHR</data>
</node>
<node id="FOXO3">
  <data key="d1">FOXO3</data>
  <data key="d2">FOXO3a</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Forkhead transcription factor driving pro-apoptotic and stress-response genes; one of the AKT substrates whose phosphorylation depends disproportionately on mTORC2-mediated Ser473 phosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d7">FOXO3</data>
  <data key="d8">FKHRL1</data>
</node>
<node id="GCK">
  <data key="d1">GCK</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucokinase</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Hepatic hexokinase IV; induced downstream of hepatic mTORC2–AKT signalling and the entry point for hepatic glycolytic flux.</data>
  <data key="d7">GCK</data>
  <data key="d8">GK; glucokinase; hexokinase IV</data>
</node>
<node id="GLUCONEOGENESIS">
  <data key="d1">GLUCONEOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Hepatic gluconeogenesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Hepatic de novo glucose production driven by FOXO1-dependent transcription; restrained by hepatic mTORC2 through AKT-mediated FOXO1 suppression.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="MDM2">
  <data key="d1">MDM2</data>
  <data key="d2">MDM2 (E3 ubiquitin ligase)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">E3 ubiquitin ligase and principal negative regulator of p53. AKT phosphorylates MDM2 at Ser166/Ser186, promoting its nuclear entry and p53 turnover; this is the survival arm of AKT output biased by mTORC2-dependent Ser473 phosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d7">MDM2</data>
  <data key="d8">HDM2</data>
</node>
<node id="MLXIPL">
  <data key="d1">MLXIPL</data>
  <data key="d2">ChREBP</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Carbohydrate-response element-binding protein; adipose mTORC2 drives ChREBP-dependent de novo lipogenesis, with consequences for hepatic glucose metabolism.</data>
  <data key="d7">MLXIPL</data>
  <data key="d8">MondoB</data>
</node>
<node id="NDRG1">
  <data key="d1">NDRG1</data>
  <data key="d2">NDRG1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">N-myc downstream-regulated gene 1 product; SGK1 substrate at Thr346/356/366 and the standard cellular readout of mTORC2–SGK1 activity.</data>
  <data key="d7">NDRG1</data>
  <data key="d8">N-myc downstream regulated 1</data>
</node>
<node id="NEDD4L">
  <data key="d1">NEDD4L</data>
  <data key="d2">Nedd4-2</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">HECT-domain E3 ubiquitin ligase that ubiquitinates ENaC subunits and promotes their retrieval from the apical membrane; inactivated by SGK1 phosphorylation and 14-3-3 binding.</data>
  <data key="d7">NEDD4L</data>
  <data key="d8">NEDD4-2</data>
</node>
<node id="RAC1">
  <data key="d1">RAC1</data>
  <data key="d2">Rac1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Rho-family GTPase driving branched actin polymerisation, lamellipodia and dendritic spine remodelling; activated downstream of mTORC2 via Tiam1 and downstream of PKC signalling.</data>
  <data key="d7">RAC1</data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="RHOA">
  <data key="d1">RHOA</data>
  <data key="d2">RhoA</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Rho-family GTPase controlling stress fibres, actomyosin contractility and adhesion; part of the Rho-family signalling affected when mTORC2-dependent PKC protein is lost.</data>
  <data key="d7">RHOA</data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="SCNN1">
  <data key="d1">SCNN1</data>
  <data key="d2">ENaC</data>
  <data key="d3">complex</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Epithelial sodium channel of the aldosterone-sensitive distal nephron; principal effector through which mTORC2–SGK1 sets sodium reabsorption and blood pressure.</data>
  <data key="d7">SCNN1A, SCNN1B, SCNN1G</data>
  <data key="d8">epithelial sodium channel</data>
</node>
<node id="SLC2A4">
  <data key="d1">SLC2A4</data>
  <data key="d2">GLUT4</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Insulin-responsive glucose transporter of muscle and adipose tissue; its translocation to the plasma membrane is the proximate step in insulin-stimulated glucose uptake.</data>
  <data key="d7">SLC2A4</data>
  <data key="d8">GLUT-4</data>
</node>
<node id="TBC1D4">
  <data key="d1">TBC1D4</data>
  <data key="d2">AS160 (TBC1D4)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Rab GAP that restrains GLUT4 vesicle translocation; AKT phosphorylation at Thr642 creates a 14-3-3 site and relieves this restraint, permitting insulin-stimulated glucose uptake.</data>
  <data key="d7">TBC1D4</data>
  <data key="d8">TBC1D4</data>
</node>
<node id="TIAM1">
  <data key="d1">TIAM1</data>
  <data key="d2">Tiam1</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Rac1-specific guanine nucleotide exchange factor; the mTORC2 effector through which forebrain mTORC2 drives actin polymerisation required for consolidation of long-term memory.</data>
  <data key="d7">TIAM1</data>
  <data key="d8">T-lymphoma invasion and metastasis 1</data>
</node>
<node id="BMAL1">
  <data key="d1">BMAL1</data>
  <data key="d2">BMAL1 (ARNTL)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Core clock bHLH-PAS transcription factor; heterodimerises with CLOCK to drive E-box transcription. Also a negative regulator of mTORC1 and, when phosphorylated by S6K1, a promoter of rhythmic cap-dependent translation.</data>
  <data key="d7">ARNTL</data>
  <data key="d8">ARNTL; MOP3; brain and muscle ARNT-like 1</data>
</node>
<node id="CLOCK">
  <data key="d1">CLOCK</data>
  <data key="d2">CLOCK</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Core clock bHLH-PAS transcription factor and obligate BMAL1 partner; the CLOCK–BMAL1 heterodimer drives E-box-dependent transcription of Per, Cry and many metabolic output genes.</data>
  <data key="d7">CLOCK</data>
  <data key="d8">circadian locomotor output cycles kaput</data>
</node>
<node id="CLOCK_BMAL1">
  <data key="d1">CLOCK_BMAL1</data>
  <data key="d2">CLOCK–BMAL1 heterodimer</data>
  <data key="d3">complex</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Positive limb transcription factor of the mammalian clock; binds E-boxes to activate Per1/Per2, Cry1/Cry2 and clock-controlled output genes, and is repressed by the PER–CRY complex it induces.</data>
  <data key="d7">CLOCK/ARNTL</data>
  <data key="d8">BMAL1:CLOCK</data>
</node>
<node id="CRY1">
  <data key="d1">CRY1</data>
  <data key="d2">CRY1 (Cryptochrome 1)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Negative-limb clock protein; partners PER to repress CLOCK–BMAL1. Its stability is set by AMPK-dependent phosphorylation and FBXL3-mediated degradation, making it an energy-sensitive clock node.</data>
  <data key="d7">CRY1</data>
  <data key="d8">cryptochrome circadian regulator 1</data>
</node>
<node id="CSNK1E">
  <data key="d1">CSNK1E</data>
  <data key="d2">Casein kinase 1ε (CSNK1E)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">PER-directed kinase controlling PER2 stability and nuclear entry; the node through which AMPK (an mTOR-pathway energy sensor) reaches the clock.</data>
  <data key="d7">CSNK1E</data>
  <data key="d8">CKIepsilon; CKIε</data>
</node>
<node id="NR1D1">
  <data key="d1">NR1D1</data>
  <data key="d2">REV-ERBα (NR1D1)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Nuclear receptor of the accessory clock loop; CLOCK–BMAL1 target that represses Bmal1 transcription through RORE elements, generating the antiphase BMAL1 rhythm.</data>
  <data key="d7">NR1D1</data>
  <data key="d8">REV-ERBa; Rev-erbα</data>
</node>
<node id="PER1">
  <data key="d1">PER1</data>
  <data key="d2">PER1 (Period 1)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Negative-limb clock protein and immediate-early photic response gene in the SCN; its light-induced expression is attenuated by mTOR inhibition, linking mTOR to phase shifting.</data>
  <data key="d7">PER1</data>
  <data key="d8">Period circadian regulator 1</data>
</node>
<node id="PER_CRY_COMPLEX">
  <data key="d1">PER_CRY_COMPLEX</data>
  <data key="d2">PER–CRY repressor complex</data>
  <data key="d3">complex</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Heteromeric PER–CRY complex of the negative limb; formed in the cytoplasm, enters the nucleus and represses CLOCK–BMAL1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">PER-CRY</data>
</node>
<node id="VIP">
  <data key="d1">VIP</data>
  <data key="d2">VIP (vasoactive intestinal peptide)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Neuropeptide of SCN core neurons required for synchrony and rhythmicity of the SCN network; its translation in the SCN is under 4E-BP1 control and therefore sensitive to mTORC1 activity.</data>
  <data key="d7">VIP</data>
  <data key="d8">vasoactive intestinal polypeptide</data>
</node>
<node id="ABETA">
  <data key="d1">ABETA</data>
  <data key="d2">Amyloid-beta</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Aggregation-prone peptide whose brain burden falls with autophagy induction; rapamycin lowers it in 3xTg-AD mice when given before substantial pathology.</data>
  <data key="d7">APP</data>
  <data key="d8">Abeta</data>
</node>
<node id="HTT">
  <data key="d1">HTT</data>
  <data key="d2">Huntingtin (polyQ)</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Polyglutamine-expanded huntingtin, an autophagy substrate reduced by rapamycin or CCI-779 in fly and mouse models.</data>
  <data key="d7">HTT</data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="SNCA">
  <data key="d1">SNCA</data>
  <data key="d2">alpha-Synuclein</data>
  <data key="d3">protein</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">6</data>
  <data key="d6">Aggregation-prone synaptic protein cleared by macroautophagy.</data>
  <data key="d7">SNCA</data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="CAP_DEPENDENT_TRANSLATION">
  <data key="d1">CAP_DEPENDENT_TRANSLATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Cap-dependent translation</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">eIF4F-dependent initiation on 5'-capped mRNAs, preferentially affecting transcripts with structured 5' UTRs; only partially suppressed by rapalogs because Thr37/Thr46 of 4E-BP1 resist them.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">cap-dependent initiation; protein synthesis rhythm</data>
</node>
<node id="CELL_GROWTH">
  <data key="d1">CELL_GROWTH</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell growth (mass/size)</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Increase in cell mass and size driven by the S6K-rpS6 axis and ribosome biogenesis; distinct from proliferation, which depends on the 4E-BP arm.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">cell size</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CELL_PROLIFERATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CELL_PROLIFERATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell proliferation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">mTORC1-dependent proliferation controlled by the 4E-BP-eIF4E arm (cyclin D1, c-Myc translation) rather than by S6K-dependent cell growth.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">proliferation</data>
</node>
<node id="PROTEIN_SYNTHESIS">
  <data key="d1">PROTEIN_SYNTHESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Protein synthesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">mTORC1-dependent anabolic response of skeletal muscle to loading and to essential amino acids; blocked in humans by acute rapamycin, and the basis of the anabolic-resistance concern in older adults.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">muscle protein synthesis; translation</data>
</node>
<node id="RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS">
  <data key="d1">RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Ribosome biogenesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Coordinated production of rRNA (Pol I), 5S rRNA and tRNA (Pol III) and ribosomal proteins (Pol II/TOP translation) scaled by mTORC1 and S6K1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">ribosomal biogenesis</data>
</node>
<node id="ADIPOGENESIS">
  <data key="d1">ADIPOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">adipogenesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Differentiation and lipid-storage programme of adipocytes requiring mTORC1; adipocyte Raptor deletion impairs adipogenesis and reduces fat mass.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="AUTOPHAGIC_LYSOSOME_REFORMATION">
  <data key="d1">AUTOPHAGIC_LYSOSOME_REFORMATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Autophagic lysosome reformation</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Tubulation and scission of autolysosomes that regenerates functional lysosomes at the end of an autophagic cycle; it requires mTOR reactivation driven by degradation-derived amino acids [686]. This reactivation is the mechanistic basis of the oscillatory behaviour of mTORC1 activity during prolonged autophagy.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">ALR</data>
</node>
<node id="AUTOPHAGY">
  <data key="d1">AUTOPHAGY</data>
  <data key="d2">autophagy</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Macroautophagic sequestration and lysosomal degradation of cytoplasmic material; one of the two principal catabolic arms repressed by mTORC1, and the arm with greatest consequence in post-mitotic tissue.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">autophagic flux; lysosomal flux; macroautophagy</data>
</node>
<node id="CHOLESTEROL_SYNTHESIS">
  <data key="d1">CHOLESTEROL_SYNTHESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">cholesterol synthesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">SREBP2-driven sterol biosynthetic programme requiring mTOR activity; partially rapamycin-resistant at the transcriptional level.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">sterol biosynthesis</data>
</node>
<node id="CLEAR_NETWORK">
  <data key="d1">CLEAR_NETWORK</data>
  <data key="d2">CLEAR gene network</data>
  <data key="d3">transcription_program</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Coordinated Lysosomal Expression And Regulation network of approximately 470 lysosomal and autophagy genes driven by TFEB/TFE3 through a palindromic E-box promoter motif.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">CLEAR network</data>
</node>
<node id="GLUTAMINE_ANAPLEROSIS">
  <data key="d1">GLUTAMINE_ANAPLEROSIS</data>
  <data key="d2">glutamine anaplerosis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Replenishment of TCA-cycle intermediates from glutamine-derived alpha-ketoglutarate, supported downstream of mTORC1 by the S6K1-GDH axis and by c-Myc.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">glutaminolysis</data>
</node>
<node id="GLUTATHIONE_SYNTHESIS">
  <data key="d1">GLUTATHIONE_SYNTHESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">glutathione synthesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">ATF4-dependent production of glutathione downstream of mTORC1, linking growth signalling to redox buffering alongside protein synthesis.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">GSH synthesis</data>
</node>
<node id="GLYCOLYSIS">
  <data key="d1">GLYCOLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d2">glycolysis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Glycolytic flux; supported by hepatic mTORC2 via AKT and glucokinase, and required (with glutaminolysis) for effector T-cell metabolic reprogramming.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">Warburg-type glycolysis; glycolytic reprogramming</data>
</node>
<node id="LIPOGENESIS">
  <data key="d1">LIPOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">de novo lipogenesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">SREBP1-driven fatty-acid synthesis downstream of mTORC1; in liver it is the basis of mTORC1-associated steatosis and of rapalog-associated dyslipidaemia.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">fatty acid synthesis</data>
</node>
<node id="LYSOSOME_BIOGENESIS">
  <data key="d1">LYSOSOME_BIOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">lysosomal biogenesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">TFEB/TFE3-driven expansion of lysosomal number and degradative capacity through the CLEAR network; suppressed by mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="MITOCHONDRIAL_BIOGENESIS">
  <data key="d1">MITOCHONDRIAL_BIOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">mitochondrial biogenesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Expansion of mitochondrial mass and oxidative capacity supported by 4E-BP-dependent translation of nuclear-encoded mitochondrial mRNAs and by the YY1-PGC-1alpha transcriptional complex.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="ONE_CARBON_METABOLISM">
  <data key="d1">ONE_CARBON_METABOLISM</data>
  <data key="d2">one-carbon/folate metabolism</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Mitochondrial tetrahydrofolate cycle supplying formyl and methyl units for nucleotide synthesis and methylation reactions; coupled to mTORC1 through ATF4-MTHFD2.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">folate cycle</data>
</node>
<node id="PENTOSE_PHOSPHATE_PATHWAY">
  <data key="d1">PENTOSE_PHOSPHATE_PATHWAY</data>
  <data key="d2">pentose phosphate pathway</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Oxidative branch activated through SREBP-driven G6PD expression; supplies NADPH for reductive biosynthesis and ribose-5-phosphate for nucleotides.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">PPP</data>
</node>
<node id="PURINE_SYNTHESIS">
  <data key="d1">PURINE_SYNTHESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">de novo purine synthesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Purine nucleotide production driven by mTORC1 through ATF4-dependent control of the mitochondrial tetrahydrofolate cycle.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PYRIMIDINE_SYNTHESIS">
  <data key="d1">PYRIMIDINE_SYNTHESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">de novo pyrimidine synthesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Pyrimidine nucleotide production stimulated acutely by the S6K1-CAD Ser1859 axis downstream of mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="SASP">
  <data key="d1">SASP</data>
  <data key="d2">senescence-associated secretory phenotype</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Pro-inflammatory secretome of senescent cells, which requires mTORC1-driven translation (IL1A; MAPKAPK2); rapamycin suppresses the SASP without eliminating senescent cells, an example of geroprotective benefit without causal primacy.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">SASP</data>
</node>
<node id="ACTIN_CYTOSKELETON">
  <data key="d1">ACTIN_CYTOSKELETON</data>
  <data key="d2">Actin cytoskeleton</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Actin filament organisation and polymerisation; the founding mTORC2-dependent output, driven through Rho-family GTPases, PKC isoforms and Tiam1–Rac1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="CELL_ADHESION">
  <data key="d1">CELL_ADHESION</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell adhesion</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Cell–matrix and cell–cell adhesion dynamics downstream of PKC and Rho-family signalling, reduced in mTORC2-deficient cells.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="CELL_MIGRATION">
  <data key="d1">CELL_MIGRATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell migration</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Directed cell motility dependent on cytoskeletal remodelling; impaired when mTORC2-dependent PKC protein and Rho-family signalling are lost.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="CELL_SURVIVAL">
  <data key="d1">CELL_SURVIVAL</data>
  <data key="d2">Cell survival signalling</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Anti-apoptotic programme sustained by AKT phosphorylation of BAD and of FOXO transcription factors; the arm of AKT output most dependent on mTORC2-mediated Ser473 phosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="EPITHELIAL_POLARITY">
  <data key="d1">EPITHELIAL_POLARITY</data>
  <data key="d2">Epithelial polarity</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Apicobasal organisation of epithelial sheets, established largely through atypical PKC in the Par3–Par6 module and therefore dependent on mTORC2-mediated PKC maturation.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="GLUCOSE_UPTAKE">
  <data key="d1">GLUCOSE_UPTAKE</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin-stimulated glucose uptake</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">GLUT4-mediated glucose entry into muscle and adipose tissue following insulin; reduced by adipose Rictor deletion and by chronic mTORC2 loss.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="ION_TRANSPORT">
  <data key="d1">ION_TRANSPORT</data>
  <data key="d2">Distal nephron ion transport</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Transepithelial sodium, potassium and chloride handling in the distal nephron, set by SGK1-dependent regulation of ENaC, the Na+/K+-ATPase and CLC-K channels.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="LIPOLYSIS">
  <data key="d1">LIPOLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Lipolysis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Hydrolysis of stored triacylglycerol in adipocytes. An adipose mTORC2-dependent output alongside insulin-stimulated glucose uptake; adipocyte Rictor deletion alters lipid handling.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="LTP_LATE_PHASE">
  <data key="d1">LTP_LATE_PHASE</data>
  <data key="d2">Late-phase LTP (L-LTP)</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Protein-synthesis-dependent late phase of hippocampal long-term potentiation and the long-term memory that depends on it; rapamycin-sensitive, whereas early-phase LTP and short-term memory are spared.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">L-LTP</data>
</node>
<node id="MYELINATION">
  <data key="d1">MYELINATION</data>
  <data key="d2">CNS myelination</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Formation and maintenance of oligodendrocyte myelin sheaths in the central nervous system. Oligodendrocyte-lineage deletion separates the complexes: Raptor loss causes persistent hypomyelination, Rictor loss has modest and largely transient effects, and only the double mutant impairs oligodendrocyte differentiation.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TH2_DIFFERENTIATION">
  <data key="d1">TH2_DIFFERENTIATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Th2 effector differentiation</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into Th2 effector cells. Selectively dependent on Rictor-mTORC2, in contrast to Th1/Th17 differentiation, which depends on Rheb-mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TREG_DIFFERENTIATION">
  <data key="d1">TREG_DIFFERENTIATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Foxp3+ regulatory T cell differentiation</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Differentiation of naive CD4+ T cells into Foxp3+ regulatory T cells. Restrained by both mTOR complexes; loss of mTOR kinase activity promotes it, which is the basis of preferential Treg expansion under rapamycin.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">Treg differentiation</data>
</node>
<node id="CIRCADIAN_CLOCK">
  <data key="d1">CIRCADIAN_CLOCK</data>
  <data key="d2">Circadian clock (transcription–translation feedback loop)</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Cell-autonomous ~24 h oscillator built from the CLOCK–BMAL1 activator limb and the PER/CRY repressor limb; entrained centrally by light and peripherally by feeding, and reciprocally coupled to mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">TTFL; molecular clock</data>
</node>
<node id="GLUTAMINOLYSIS">
  <data key="d1">GLUTAMINOLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Glutaminolysis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Glutamine catabolism supporting anaplerosis; part of the metabolic reprogramming of effector T-cell activation that requires both mTOR complexes.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="INSULIN_SECRETION">
  <data key="d1">INSULIN_SECRETION</data>
  <data key="d2">Insulin secretion</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Glucose-stimulated insulin release from pancreatic beta-cells. Insulin secretion and beta-cell survival are the mTORC2-dependent outputs of the beta-cell; reduced secretion is part of the mechanism of rapamycin-associated NODAT.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="KETOGENESIS">
  <data key="d1">KETOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Ketogenesis</data>
  <data key="d3">process</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">7</data>
  <data key="d6">Hepatic production of ketone bodies during fasting; a liver mTORC1-dependent output. mTORC1 suppresses fasting ketogenesis via PPARalpha/NCoR1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_BHD">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_BHD</data>
  <data key="d2">Birt-Hogg-Dube syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">lysosomal_nutrient_sensing</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Autosomal dominant syndrome of fibrofolliculomas, pulmonary cysts with pneumothorax and renal tumours caused by FLCN loss.</data>
  <data key="d7">FLCN</data>
  <data key="d8">BHD</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_GATOR1_EPILEPSY">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_GATOR1_EPILEPSY</data>
  <data key="d2">GATOR1-related focal epilepsy (FFEVF)</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">lysosomal_nutrient_sensing</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Familial focal epilepsies caused by heterozygous loss-of-function variants in DEPDC5, NPRL2 or NPRL3, with elevated SUDEP risk; everolimus titrated to troughs of 5-15 ng/mL gave genotype-dependent, mixed results in small open-label series. Graded emerging, in explicit contrast to established everolimus in TSC.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">FFEVF; GATOR1 epilepsy</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_SZT2_ENCEPHALOPATHY">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_SZT2_ENCEPHALOPATHY</data>
  <data key="d2">SZT2-related developmental and epileptic encephalopathy</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">lysosomal_nutrient_sensing</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Infantile encephalopathy with epilepsy, intellectual disability, macrocephaly and a dysmorphic corpus callosum, caused by biallelic SZT2 mutations.</data>
  <data key="d7">SZT2</data>
  <data key="d8">DEE18; SZT2 syndrome</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_AGE_MEMORY_IMPAIRMENT">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_AGE_MEMORY_IMPAIRMENT</data>
  <data key="d2">Age-associated memory impairment</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Decline in long-term memory with age; reversed in mice and flies by a small molecule that raises mTORC2 activity, which also enhances L-LTP in wild-type animals.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_BODY_GROWTH">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_BODY_GROWTH</data>
  <data key="d2">Whole-body growth</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Organismal body size and growth trajectory. Adipose-tissue mTORC2 negatively controls whole-body growth, so adipocyte Rictor deletion increases body size - a direction of effect opposite to the usual anabolic framing of mTOR signalling.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_GLUCOSE_INTOLERANCE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_GLUCOSE_INTOLERANCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Glucose intolerance / insulin resistance</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Impaired systemic glucose handling arising chiefly from mTORC2 loss: liver-specific Rictor deletion produces a type-2-diabetes-like phenotype, and chronic rapamycin reproduces it pharmacologically.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HYPOTENSION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HYPOTENSION</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypotension</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Low systemic arterial pressure resulting from impaired distal nephron sodium reabsorption. Reported together with salt wasting on renal mTORC2 loss, downstream of failed SGK1-dependent activation of ENaC, Na+/K+-ATPase and CLC-K.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">low blood pressure</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_LONG_TERM_MEMORY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_LONG_TERM_MEMORY</data>
  <data key="d2">Long-term memory consolidation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Persistent memory formation requiring late-phase LTP; selectively impaired by forebrain Rictor deletion while short-term memory is spared.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SALT_WASTING">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SALT_WASTING</data>
  <data key="d2">Salt wasting and hypotension</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Distal-nephron phenotype of renal mTORC2 loss, mediated by failure of SGK1-dependent ENaC and Na+/K+-ATPase regulation; the predicted electrolyte disturbance of mTORC2 inhibition in kidney.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">renal salt wasting</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_BETA_CELL">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_BETA_CELL</data>
  <data key="d2">Pancreatic β-cell</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Islet cell type in which both complexes are required; β-cell Rictor deletion causes mild hyperglycaemia with reduced β-cell mass, lower AKT Ser473 phosphorylation and nuclear FOXO1 accumulation.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_CNS_NEURON">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_CNS_NEURON</data>
  <data key="d2">CNS neuron</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Forebrain neurons in which mTORC2 supports actin-dependent consolidation of long-term memory and spine density; the only tissue where mTORC2 loss produces a cognitive rather than metabolic deficit.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_HEART">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_HEART</data>
  <data key="d2">Cardiomyocyte</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Cardiac muscle cell in which mTORC2 is cytoprotective during ischaemia–reperfusion through AKT-dependent anti-apoptotic signalling, so that dual inhibitors are predicted to be more cardiotoxic.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_KIDNEY_TUBULE">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_KIDNEY_TUBULE</data>
  <data key="d2">Renal tubule (distal nephron)</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Aldosterone-sensitive distal nephron, where mTORC2–SGK1 signalling governs sodium handling; tubular mTORC2 loss produces salt wasting and hypotension.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_SKELETAL_MUSCLE">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_SKELETAL_MUSCLE</data>
  <data key="d2">Skeletal muscle</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Tissue in which mTORC1 is dominant and mTORC2 largely dispensable: muscle-specific Rictor deletion is close to phenotypically silent while Raptor deletion causes dystrophy.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_SKS">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_SKS</data>
  <data key="d2">Smith–Kingsmore syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Germline gain-of-function MTOR mTORopathy with macrocephaly, intellectual disability and seizures; its phenotype has been extended to include sleep–wake and circadian disturbance, hyperphagia and hyperactivity.</data>
  <data key="d7">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d8">SKS</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CIRCADIAN_AMPLITUDE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CIRCADIAN_AMPLITUDE</data>
  <data key="d2">Cellular circadian amplitude</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Amplitude of the cellular circadian oscillation, dampened by pathogenic MTOR variants in Smith-Kingsmore syndrome and improved by low-dose rapamycin in engineered U2OS cells expressing SKS-associated MTOR variants.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">clock amplitude</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CIRCADIAN_BEHAVIOUR_ABNORMALITY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CIRCADIAN_BEHAVIOUR_ABNORMALITY</data>
  <data key="d2">Circadian behavioural abnormality</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Disrupted period, phase or consolidation of locomotor rhythms in rodent models of mTOR hyperactivation; rescued by reducing Bmal1 gene dosage, implicating BMAL1 proteostasis as the mediating lesion.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">abnormal locomotor rhythm</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CIRCADIAN_PERIOD">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CIRCADIAN_PERIOD</data>
  <data key="d2">Circadian period length</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Free-running period of molecular and behavioural rhythms; lengthened by mTOR inhibition in SCN explants, fibroblasts and mice [484].</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">free-running period; tau</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_DELAYED_SLEEP_WAKE_PHASE_DISORDER">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_DELAYED_SLEEP_WAKE_PHASE_DISORDER</data>
  <data key="d2">Delayed sleep–wake phase disorder</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Circadian rhythm sleep disorder in which habitual sleep onset and offset are shifted substantially later than conventional times; precipitated in some Smith–Kingsmore patients by high-dose rapamycin.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">DSWPD; delayed sleep-wake phase</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HYPERACTIVITY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HYPERACTIVITY</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperactivity</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Recognised feature of Smith–Kingsmore syndrome in the same cohort [361], reported alongside sleep–wake disturbance.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HYPERPHAGIA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HYPERPHAGIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperphagia</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Recognised feature of Smith–Kingsmore syndrome in the 28-individual cohort [361]; mechanistically expected from hypothalamic mTORC1 nutrient sensing.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_PHOTIC_ENTRAINMENT">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_PHOTIC_ENTRAINMENT</data>
  <data key="d2">Photic entrainment</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Light-induced phase shifting and re-entrainment of behavioural rhythms; an mTOR-modulated output [79,484].</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">light entrainment; phase shifting</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_PREMATURE_AGEING">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_PREMATURE_AGEING</data>
  <data key="d2">Premature ageing phenotype</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Accelerated age-related pathology (cataracts, organ atrophy, shortened lifespan) seen in Bmal1-null mice and attributed in part to unrestrained mTORC1 signalling.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">accelerated ageing</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SLEEP_WAKE_DISTURBANCE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SLEEP_WAKE_DISTURBANCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Sleep–wake disturbance</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Disturbed sleep timing, maintenance and quality; a recognised feature of Smith–Kingsmore syndrome and a proposed monitoring endpoint for mTOR-directed therapy.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">delayed sleep-wake phase disorder; sleep disturbance</data>
</node>
<node id="SCN">
  <data key="d1">SCN</data>
  <data key="d2">Suprachiasmatic nucleus</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">circadian</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Hypothalamic master pacemaker; receives retinal photic input, synchronises peripheral clocks, and depends on VIP signalling for network synchrony. mTOR signalling here modulates photic entrainment and central clock function.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">SCN; master clock</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_AD">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_AD</data>
  <data key="d2">Alzheimer's disease</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Target of the autophagy-based mTOR-inhibition rationale; supported by 3xTg-AD mouse data but with no controlled human efficacy data - the ERAP phase IIa study of weekly oral rapamycin uses FDG-PET cerebral glucose metabolism as its primary outcome.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">AD</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_ANGIOMYOLIPOMA">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_ANGIOMYOLIPOMA</data>
  <data key="d2">Renal angiomyolipoma</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">TSC-associated (or sporadic LAM-associated) renal tumour; EXIST-2 indication, with 42% of everolimus-treated patients achieving at least 50% reduction.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">AML</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_BREAST_CANCER">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_BREAST_CANCER</data>
  <data key="d2">HR+/HER2- advanced breast cancer</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Hormone receptor-positive, HER2-negative advanced breast cancer progressing on an aromatase inhibitor; the single disease in which the mTOR, PI3K and AKT nodes have been tested in separate randomised trials.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">breast cancer</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_CANCER">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_CANCER</data>
  <data key="d2">Cancer (advanced solid tumours)</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Mixed population of advanced solid tumours, 58% carrying pathogenic mTOR-pathway variants, enrolled in the first-in-human bi-steric inhibitor study.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">solid tumour</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_ENDOMETRIAL_CANCER">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_ENDOMETRIAL_CANCER</data>
  <data key="d2">Endometrial carcinoma</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Carcinoma with PIK3CA mutation in roughly 30% and PTEN loss in roughly 35% of cases; site of the complete response to RMC-5552.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_FCD2">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_FCD2</data>
  <data key="d2">Focal cortical dysplasia type II</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Cortical malformation from somatic mosaic activating variants confined to the dysplastic cortex, with drug-resistant focal epilepsy treated surgically.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">FCD II</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_FSGS">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_FSGS</data>
  <data key="d2">Focal segmental glomerulosclerosis</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Glomerular scarring produced in mice by podocyte Tsc1 deletion, the upper limb of the podocyte Goldilocks requirement.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">FSGS</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_GLIOBLASTOMA">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_GLIOBLASTOMA</data>
  <data key="d2">Glioblastoma</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">PTEN is lost in approximately 40% of glioblastomas, the highest frequency of PTEN loss among these tumour types.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">GBM</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_HD">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_HD</data>
  <data key="d2">Huntington's disease</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Polyglutamine expansion disorder; mTOR inhibition by rapamycin or the ester analogue CCI-779 (temsirolimus) induces autophagy and reduces polyglutamine toxicity in fly and mouse models.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">HD</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_HME">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_HME</data>
  <data key="d2">Hemimegalencephaly</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Hemispheric brain enlargement with severe epilepsy from somatic mosaic activation of MTOR, PIK3CA or AKT3; mTOR inhibition investigational.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">HME</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_LAM">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_LAM</data>
  <data key="d2">Lymphangioleiomyomatosis</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Progressive cystic lung disease driven by TSC2-deficient smooth-muscle-like cells, sporadic or TSC-associated; an approved sirolimus indication.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">LAM</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_NET">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_NET</data>
  <data key="d2">Neuroendocrine tumour</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Well-differentiated pancreatic, gastrointestinal and lung neuroendocrine tumours; a lineage dependency without a defined pathway lesion, treated with everolimus on RADIANT-3 and RADIANT-4.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">NET; gastrointestinal and lung NET; pNET; pancreatic NET</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PD">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PD</data>
  <data key="d2">Parkinson's disease</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Alpha-synuclein proteinopathy; rapamycin protects against neuron death in in vitro and in vivo models, with autophagic clearance as the proposed mechanism. Preclinical only.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">PD</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PECOMA">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PECOMA</data>
  <data key="d2">Malignant PEComa</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Malignant PEComa; the nab-sirolimus indication from AMPECT, with response enriched in TSC2-mutant tumours.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">PEComa; perivascular epithelioid cell tumour</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PHTS">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PHTS</data>
  <data key="d2">PTEN hamartoma tumour syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Germline PTEN loss spanning Cowden and Bannayan-Riley-Ruvalcaba syndromes, with hamartomas, macrocephaly and elevated breast, thyroid and endometrial cancer risk.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">Cowden syndrome</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PJS">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PJS</data>
  <data key="d2">Peutz-Jeghers syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Germline STK11 (LKB1) loss causing hamartomatous gastrointestinal polyposis, mucocutaneous pigmentation and elevated cancer risk.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">PJS</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PKD">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PKD</data>
  <data key="d2">Polycystic kidney disease</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Cystic kidney disease in which elevated mTORC1 activity contributes to cyst growth; trials limited by dose-related toxicity rather than absent rationale.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">ADPKD</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PMSE">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PMSE</data>
  <data key="d2">PMSE syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Polyhydramnios, megalencephaly and symptomatic epilepsy syndrome; autosomal recessive STRADA loss with impaired LKB1-AMPK signalling and mTORC1 hyperactivation; rapamycin reduces seizures (preclinical and small clinical evidence).</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">PMSE; Pretzel syndrome</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PROS">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PROS</data>
  <data key="d2">PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Mosaic somatic PIK3CA activating variants causing segmental overgrowth (CLOVES, megalencephaly-capillary malformation, isolated macrodactyly)</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">CLOVES; PROS</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PROSTATE_CANCER">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PROSTATE_CANCER</data>
  <data key="d2">Prostate carcinoma</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">PTEN is lost in approximately 30% of prostate cancers.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_PROTEUS">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_PROTEUS</data>
  <data key="d2">Proteus syndrome</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Mosaic overgrowth disorder caused by the somatic activating AKT1 E17K variant</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_RCC">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_RCC</data>
  <data key="d2">Renal cell carcinoma</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Clear-cell renal carcinoma; an approved rapalog indication in which benefit plausibly runs through HIF-dependent translation and the angiogenic programme rather than a cell-autonomous mTORC1 dependency.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">RCC</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_SEGA">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_SEGA</data>
  <data key="d2">Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">TSC-associated low-grade glioneuronal tumour; the EXIST-1 indication, with roughly 35% of everolimus-treated patients achieving at least 50% volume reduction versus 0% on placebo.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">SEGA</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_TSC">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_TSC</data>
  <data key="d2">Tuberous sclerosis complex</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Germline TSC1 or TSC2 loss with somatic second hits producing SEGA, angiomyolipoma, LAM and epilepsy; the indication in which the rapalog mechanistic rationale is fully borne out.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">TSC</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_UROTHELIAL_CARCINOMA">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_UROTHELIAL_CARCINOMA</data>
  <data key="d2">Urothelial carcinoma</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Bladder cancer providing the exceptional-responder evidence for proximal-brake dependency, with TSC1 mutations in roughly 8% of a 109-tumour series.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">bladder cancer</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_ANABOLIC_RESISTANCE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_ANABOLIC_RESISTANCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Anabolic resistance</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Blunted muscle protein synthesis response to amino acids and loading in ageing muscle, with elevated basal signalling and attenuated acute response: a compressed dynamic range.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_ASTROCYTE_PROLIFERATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_ASTROCYTE_PROLIFERATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Astrocyte proliferation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Reactive astrocytosis accompanying microglial Tsc1 deletion, alongside reduced synapse density and early-onset spontaneous recurrent seizures.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">astrocytosis</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_AUTISM_LIKE_BEHAVIOUR">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_AUTISM_LIKE_BEHAVIOUR</data>
  <data key="d2">Autism-like behaviour</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Social-interaction and repetitive-behaviour deficits produced by 4E-BP2 loss, demonstrating that excessive as well as deficient translational signalling is deleterious in neurons.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_BETA_CELL_FAILURE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_BETA_CELL_FAILURE</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta-cell dedifferentiation and failure</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Initial expansion of mass and secretion under chronic mTORC1 hyperactivation followed by dedifferentiation through ER stress and suppressed autophagy.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_BETA_CELL_MASS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_BETA_CELL_MASS</data>
  <data key="d2">Beta-cell mass and maturation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Functional beta-cell number and maturity; impaired by beta-cell Raptor deletion and reduced, with elevated p27, by beta-cell Rictor deletion.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CARDIAC_HYPERTROPHY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CARDIAC_HYPERTROPHY</data>
  <data key="d2">Physiological cardiac hypertrophy</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Adaptive cardiomyocyte growth supported by mTORC1; sustained activity instead contributes to maladaptive remodelling.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CARDIOPROTECTION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CARDIOPROTECTION</data>
  <data key="d2">Cardiomyocyte cytoprotection</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">AKT-dependent anti-apoptotic protection of cardiomyocytes during ischaemia-reperfusion, supplied by mTORC2.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CD8_MEMORY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CD8_MEMORY</data>
  <data key="d2">CD8 memory T cell quantity and quality</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Number and functional quality of memory CD8 T cells generated after acute viral infection; enhanced rather than suppressed by low-dose rapamycin, T cell-intrinsically and across species.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">memory CD8 T cells</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CELLULAR_SENESCENCE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CELLULAR_SENESCENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Cellular senescence</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Stable cell-cycle arrest with an inflammatory secretome; rapamycin attenuates the consequences of senescence without eliminating senescent cells, so efficacy of an intervention does not establish causal primacy.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CYTOPENIA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CYTOPENIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Cytopenia</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Anaemia, neutropenia and thrombocytopenia on rapalogs, attributed to the proliferative requirement for mTORC1 in haematopoiesis.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">anaemia; neutropenia; thrombocytopenia</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_DILATED_CARDIOMYOPATHY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_DILATED_CARDIOMYOPATHY</data>
  <data key="d2">Dilated cardiomyopathy</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Fatal phenotype of complete cardiomyocyte mTOR deletion, marking the lower bound of the cardiac therapeutic window.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_FAT_MASS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_FAT_MASS</data>
  <data key="d2">Fat mass</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Whole-body adiposity; reduced in adipocyte-specific Raptor-null mice, which are lean with enhanced mitochondrial respiration.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_FOOD_INTAKE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_FOOD_INTAKE</data>
  <data key="d2">Food intake</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Hypothalamic (arcuate nucleus) control of feeding; intracerebroventricular leucine and leptin suppress food intake through mTORC1, rapamycin abolishes that suppression, and rapamycin partially corrects the hyperphagic obesity of POMC-neuron Tsc1 deletion.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">appetite</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HEPATIC_STEATOSIS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HEPATIC_STEATOSIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Hepatic steatosis</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Hepatic triglyceride accumulation driven by SREBP1c lipogenesis under hyperinsulinaemia; paradoxically prevented by liver-specific Tsc1 deletion.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">fatty liver</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HYPERPHAGIC_OBESITY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HYPERPHAGIC_OBESITY</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperphagic obesity</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Overeating with weight gain produced by Tsc1 deletion in POMC neurons and partially corrected by rapamycin; mechanistically expected in Smith-Kingsmore syndrome.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">hyperphagia</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HYPERTRIGLYCERIDAEMIA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HYPERTRIGLYCERIDAEMIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypertriglyceridaemia and hypercholesterolaemia</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Dose-related elevation of triglycerides and cholesterol on mTOR inhibitors, reflecting altered hepatic and adipose lipid handling by both complexes.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">dyslipidaemia</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_IMMUNOSENESCENCE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_IMMUNOSENESCENCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Immunosenescence</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Thymic involution, naive repertoire contraction, PD-1+ exhausted-like cells and inflammaging; the most productive clinical testing ground for geroprotection.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">inflammaging</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_INSULIN_RESISTANCE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_INSULIN_RESISTANCE</data>
  <data key="d2">Peripheral insulin resistance</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Reduced insulin-stimulated glucose disposal in peripheral tissues. Distinct from direct beta-cell toxicity: chronic mTORC2 loss produces peripheral insulin resistance, and the two mechanisms together account for rapamycin-associated new-onset diabetes at immunosuppressive exposures.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_ISG_EXPRESSION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_ISG_EXPRESSION</data>
  <data key="d2">Interferon-stimulated antiviral gene expression</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Upregulation of interferon-stimulated antiviral genes reported with RTB101 plus low-dose everolimus; the mechanistic correlate of the reduced laboratory-confirmed respiratory infections in that phase 2b.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">ISG</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_LEAN_MASS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_LEAN_MASS</data>
  <data key="d2">Lean tissue mass</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Fat-free body mass</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_LIFESPAN_EXTENSION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_LIFESPAN_EXTENSION</data>
  <data key="d2">Lifespan extension</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Increase in median and maximal lifespan in genetically heterogeneous mice given encapsulated rapamycin from 600 days of age, reproduced across three sites and dose- and sex-dependent.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">longevity; median lifespan</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_MALADAPTIVE_REMODELLING">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_MALADAPTIVE_REMODELLING</data>
  <data key="d2">Maladaptive cardiac remodelling</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Pathological hypertrophic remodelling driven by sustained mTORC1 activity, distinct from the physiological hypertrophy that mTORC1 also supports; attenuated by partial inhibition in multiple models.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">pathological hypertrophy</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_MICROGLIAL_ACTIVATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_MICROGLIAL_ACTIVATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Amoeboid phagocytic microglial phenotype</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Amoeboid, highly proliferative and phagocytic microglial state produced by Tsc1 loss, notably without a pro-inflammatory cytokine signature.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_MUSCLE_ATROPHY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_MUSCLE_ATROPHY</data>
  <data key="d2">Denervation muscle atrophy</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Loss of myofibre mass after denervation; prevented by constitutively active AKT, establishing sufficiency of the AKT-mTOR arm for maintenance of muscle mass.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_MUSCLE_DYSTROPHY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_MUSCLE_DYSTROPHY</data>
  <data key="d2">Muscular dystrophy (Raptor-null)</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Progressive dystrophy with impaired oxidative capacity and shortened lifespan after muscle-specific Raptor deletion; the phenotype against which near-silent Rictor deletion is contrasted.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_MUSCLE_HYPERTROPHY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_MUSCLE_HYPERTROPHY</data>
  <data key="d2">Load-induced muscle hypertrophy</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Hypertrophic growth of skeletal muscle in response to IGF-1, mechanical overload or chronic stimulation; mTORC1 is necessary and sufficient and rapamycin blocks it, which is the basis of the anabolic-resistance safety concern for chronic geroprotective dosing.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_MYELINATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_MYELINATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Myelination</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Formation and thickness of myelin sheaths; requires oligodendrocyte mTORC1, with spinal cord affected more than corpus callosum.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_NEURODEGENERATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_NEURODEGENERATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Neurodegeneration</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Progressive neuronal loss with ubiquitin-positive inclusions and motor deficits, produced by neural Atg5 or Atg7 deletion alone.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_OLIGODENDROCYTE_DIFFERENTIATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_OLIGODENDROCYTE_DIFFERENTIATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Oligodendrocyte differentiation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Maturation of oligodendrocyte precursors; impaired only in the Raptor/Rictor double mutant, indicating that mTORC2 contributes but is not rate-limiting while mTORC1 remains intact.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_OSTEOBLAST_DIFFERENTIATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_OSTEOBLAST_DIFFERENTIATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Osteoblast differentiation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Bone-forming cell differentiation requiring mTOR signalling.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_OSTEOCLAST_DIFFERENTIATION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_OSTEOCLAST_DIFFERENTIATION</data>
  <data key="d2">Osteoclast differentiation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Bone-resorbing cell differentiation also requiring mTOR, which is why the net skeletal effect of inhibition is context-dependent.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_PD1_T_CELLS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_PD1_T_CELLS</data>
  <data key="d2">PD-1+ CD4 and CD8 T cell fraction</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Accumulation of PD-1-positive CD4 and CD8 T cells with age; fractions fell in older adults given low-dose everolimus before influenza vaccination.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_PROTEINURIA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_PROTEINURIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Proteinuria</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Urinary protein loss on mTOR inhibitors, reflecting the podocyte requirement for mTORC1 and disrupted podocyte autophagic flux.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_RAPALOG_SENSITIVITY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_RAPALOG_SENSITIVITY</data>
  <data key="d2">Rapalog sensitivity</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Tumour responsiveness to allosteric mTORC1 inhibition; predicted by proximal-brake lesions as enrichment rather than individual prediction, and not by distal-network lesions.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SARCOPENIA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SARCOPENIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Sarcopenia</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Age-related loss of muscle mass and function; the predicted consequence of chronic mTOR inhibition in skeletal muscle, and the central safety question for geroscience dosing.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SEIZURES">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SEIZURES</data>
  <data key="d2">Spontaneous recurrent seizures</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Epileptic seizures; the shared endpoint of the mTORopathies and of microglial Tsc1 loss, with elevated SUDEP risk in GATOR1 epilepsies.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">epilepsy</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SPINE_DENSITY">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SPINE_DENSITY</data>
  <data key="d2">Dendritic spine density</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Density of dendritic spines, which falls when forebrain mTORC2 activity and Tiam1–Rac1-dependent actin polymerisation are reduced.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SUDEP">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SUDEP</data>
  <data key="d2">Sudden unexpected death in epilepsy</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Elevated risk carried by the GATOR1-related familial focal epilepsies, which is part of why mTOR-directed therapy in this group is being pursued on limited evidence.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">SUDEP</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_TH1_TH17">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_TH1_TH17</data>
  <data key="d2">Th1 and Th17 differentiation</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Effector CD4 lineages requiring Rheb-mTORC1; their suppression is part of the immunosuppressive action of rapalogs.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_TREG_EXPANSION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_TREG_EXPANSION</data>
  <data key="d2">Regulatory T cell expansion</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Preferential survival and expansion of CD4+CD25+Foxp3+ regulatory T cells under mTOR inhibition, while effector differentiation is blocked; the basis of the immunosuppressive use of rapamycin.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">Treg; Treg expansion</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_T_CELL_SURVIVAL">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_T_CELL_SURVIVAL</data>
  <data key="d2">T-cell survival signalling</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">AKT-dependent survival output; the second mTORC2-dependent arm in T lymphocytes alongside Th2 differentiation.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_VACCINE_RESPONSE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_VACCINE_RESPONSE</data>
  <data key="d2">Influenza vaccine antibody response</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Placebo-corrected rise in haemagglutination-inhibition titre after influenza vaccination in adults aged 65 and over; the endpoint demonstrating an inverted-U dose-response to mTOR inhibition.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">haemagglutination-inhibition titre</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_ADIPOSE">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_ADIPOSE</data>
  <data key="d2">Adipose tissue</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Tissue in which adipocyte Rictor deletion reduces insulin-stimulated glucose uptake, alters ChREBP-driven lipogenesis and lipid handling, and negatively controls whole-body growth.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">adipocyte</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_BONE">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_BONE</data>
  <data key="d2">Bone</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Skeletal tissue; mTOR signalling is required for both osteoblast and osteoclast differentiation, so the net effect of inhibition is context-dependent.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">osteoblast; osteoclast</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_HAEMATOPOIETIC">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_HAEMATOPOIETIC</data>
  <data key="d2">Haematopoietic compartment</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Marrow progenitor compartment whose proliferative mTORC1 requirement accounts for rapalog cytopenias; TSC-mTOR signalling also sets stem-cell quiescence.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">haematopoiesis</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_HYPOTHALAMUS">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_HYPOTHALAMUS</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypothalamic arcuate neuron</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Arcuate neurons in which mTORC1 acts as a nutrient sensor rather than a growth regulator, integrating leucine and leptin into control of food intake.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">POMC neuron</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_KIDNEY_PODOCYTE">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_KIDNEY_PODOCYTE</data>
  <data key="d2">Kidney podocyte</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Glomerular epithelial cell with a calibrated Goldilocks requirement: both Tsc1 deletion and Raptor deletion produce injury and proteinuria.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">podocyte</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_LIVER">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_LIVER</data>
  <data key="d2">Liver</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Tissue in which the two complexes act in opposite directions on glucose output: mTORC1 drives SREBP1c lipogenesis while mTORC2 restrains gluconeogenesis through AKT–FOXO1.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">hepatocyte</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_MICROGLIA">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_MICROGLIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Microglia</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Resident CNS macrophage; Tsc1 deletion restricted to microglia produces an amoeboid proliferative phagocytic phenotype and severe early-onset seizures.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_OLIGODENDROCYTE">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_OLIGODENDROCYTE</data>
  <data key="d2">Oligodendrocyte</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Oligodendrocyte-lineage cells of the CNS. Conditional deletion cleanly separates the two complexes: mTORC1 is rate-limiting for myelination, mTORC2 contributes but is not rate-limiting while mTORC1 remains intact. The requirement is regional, spinal cord being affected more than corpus callosum.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_ORAL_EPITHELIUM">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_ORAL_EPITHELIUM</data>
  <data key="d2">Oral epithelium</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Rapidly renewing mucosal epithelium whose mTORC1 dependence accounts for rapalog stomatitis and the dose-limiting mucositis of bi-steric agents.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">oral mucosa</data>
</node>
<node id="TISSUE_T_LYMPHOCYTE">
  <data key="d1">TISSUE_T_LYMPHOCYTE</data>
  <data key="d2">T lymphocyte</data>
  <data key="d3">tissue</data>
  <data key="d4">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">T lymphocyte compartment. The only tissue in which inhibition of the pathway produces the desired rather than an adverse effect: partial mTORC1 inhibition expands Tregs and enhances CD8 memory, which is the basis of rapalog immunosuppression and of the immune-function geroscience hypothesis.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">T cell</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_ALLOGRAFT_REJECTION</data>
  <data key="d2">Renal allograft rejection</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Alloimmune destruction of a transplanted kidney; the first approved indication for sirolimus, given at continuous immunosuppressive exposures with trough monitoring.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">transplant rejection</data>
</node>
<node id="DISEASE_NODAT">
  <data key="d1">DISEASE_NODAT</data>
  <data key="d2">New-onset diabetes after transplantation</data>
  <data key="d3">disease</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Diabetes arising at immunosuppressive mTOR-inhibitor exposures; mechanistically beta-cell toxicity plus mTORC2-dependent peripheral insulin resistance</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">NODAT; post-transplant diabetes mellitus</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_AMYLOID_BETA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_AMYLOID_BETA</data>
  <data key="d2">Brain amyloid-beta burden</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Cerebral amyloid-beta accumulation, reduced by rapamycin in 3xTg-AD mice in an autophagy-dependent manner and largely only when treatment precedes substantial pathology.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">Abeta</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_ANGIOMYOLIPOMA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_ANGIOMYOLIPOMA</data>
  <data key="d2">Renal angiomyolipoma</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">TSC-associated renal tumour; 42% of patients achieved at least 50% reduction on everolimus (EXIST-2).</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE</data>
  <data key="d2">CNS drug exposure</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Concentration of an mTOR inhibitor achieved in brain relative to blood; restricted and saturable for sirolimus and everolimus, so central and peripheral exposure-response relationships do not coincide.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">brain penetration</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_FATIGUE">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_FATIGUE</data>
  <data key="d2">Fatigue</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Treatment-related fatigue, reported in 42% of patients in the RMC-5552 first-in-human dose-escalation study.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HYPERCHOLESTEROLAEMIA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HYPERCHOLESTEROLAEMIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Hypercholesterolaemia</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Elevated plasma cholesterol during mTOR-inhibitor therapy; occurs alongside hypertriglyceridaemia and is attributed to altered hepatic and adipose lipid handling (mTORC2 and mTORC1).</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_HYPERGLYCAEMIA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_HYPERGLYCAEMIA</data>
  <data key="d2">Hyperglycaemia</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Elevated blood glucose on mTOR inhibitors, attributed chiefly to mTORC2 loss (peripheral insulin resistance) with a beta-cell contribution</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">drug-induced hyperglycaemia</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_IMPAIRED_WOUND_HEALING">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_IMPAIRED_WOUND_HEALING</data>
  <data key="d2">Impaired wound healing</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Delayed surgical healing and lymphocele formation on mTOR inhibitors, attributed to suppressed angiogenesis and fibroblast proliferation; managed by perioperative interruption.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_INFECTION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_INFECTION</data>
  <data key="d2">Infection</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Increased infection risk at immunosuppressive exposures, following from suppression of effector lymphocyte expansion; dose- and schedule-dependent and not seen consistently at low intermittent exposures.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">opportunistic infection</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_NAUSEA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_NAUSEA</data>
  <data key="d2">Nausea</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Treatment-related nausea, the second most frequent adverse event (44%) in the RMC-5552 first-in-human dose-escalation study.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_PNEUMONITIS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_PNEUMONITIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Non-infectious pneumonitis</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Non-infectious interstitial lung inflammation in roughly 5-15% of oncology patients receiving everolimus; the most serious and least mechanistically understood rapalog toxicity.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">drug-induced pneumonitis</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_PROTEIN_AGGREGATES">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_PROTEIN_AGGREGATES</data>
  <data key="d2">Misfolded protein aggregates</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Aggregation-prone substrates cleared by macroautophagy (alpha-synuclein, polyglutamine-expanded huntingtin), reduced by rapamycin or temsirolimus in fly and mouse models.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_RESPIRATORY_INFECTION">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_RESPIRATORY_INFECTION</data>
  <data key="d2">Respiratory tract infection</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Respiratory infections in older adults; ascertainment differs between participant-reported events in the phase 2a study and laboratory-confirmed events in phase 2b.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SEGA">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SEGA</data>
  <data key="d2">Subependymal giant cell astrocytoma</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">TSC-associated brain tumour; approximately 35% of patients achieved at least 50% volume reduction on everolimus versus 0% on placebo (EXIST-1).</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">SEGA</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_STOMATITIS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_STOMATITIS</data>
  <data key="d2">Stomatitis / mucositis</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Painful oral ulceration affecting roughly 30-60% of patients on rapalogs and 49% in the RMC-5552 phase 1 study; reflects mTORC1 dependence of renewing oral epithelium and is largely preventable.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8">aphthous stomatitis; mucositis</data>
</node>
<node id="PHENO_SYMPTOMATIC_RESPIRATORY_ILLNESS">
  <data key="d1">PHENO_SYMPTOMATIC_RESPIRATORY_ILLNESS</data>
  <data key="d2">Clinically symptomatic respiratory illness</data>
  <data key="d3">phenotype</data>
  <data key="d4">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d5">8</data>
  <data key="d6">Self-reported symptomatic respiratory illness; the subjective phase 3 endpoint that RTB101 monotherapy failed to meet in 1024 older adults.</data>
  <data key="d7"></data>
  <data key="d8"></data>
</node>
