  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Resistance exercise and mechanical overload activate mTORC1 in skeletal muscle, and rapamycin blocks the contraction-induced rise in human muscle protein synthesis. The proximal mechanism involves a rise in phosphatidic acid together with membrane perturbation and focal-adhesion signalling, but the enzyme generating that phosphatidic acid is disputed, with early work implicating phospholipase D and later work diacylglycerol kinase zeta.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
