<edge source="GLUCOSE" target="RAG_HETERODIMER" id="sensing:cfb74b9e9287d38f">
  <data key="d9">GLUCOSE</data>
  <data key="d10">RAG_HETERODIMER</data>
  <data key="d11">sensing</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Glucose sufficiency is relayed to mTORC1 through the Rag GTPases largely independently of AMPK (constitutively active RagA mice lose glucose sensing); glycolytic DHAP has been proposed as the sensed metabolite.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">155; 440</data>
  <data key="d17">neonatal fasting; cultured cells</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The direct glucose/DHAP sensor upstream of the Rags is unknown.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GLUCOSE" target="GLYCOLYSIS" id="metabolic_conversion:f5b3e1046f3245f7">
  <data key="d9">GLUCOSE</data>
  <data key="d10">GLYCOLYSIS</data>
  <data key="d11">metabolic_conversion</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Glucose supplies carbon for glycolysis after phosphorylation to glucose-6-phosphate.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">154</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GLUCOSE_DEPRIVATION" target="AMPK" id="sensing:7d0eb64b86700872">
  <data key="d9">GLUCOSE_DEPRIVATION</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">sensing</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Withdrawal of glucose activates AMPK through LKB1-dependent phosphorylation of Thr172: the canonical route follows the fall in ATP and the rise in AMP and ADP, while a parallel route in which unoccupied aldolase senses the loss of fructose-1,6-bisphosphate has been reported to act before adenine nucleotide ratios change.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">517; 698</data>
  <data key="d17">glucose starvation</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The aldolase route is reported by a single laboratory, whereas activation of AMPK by glucose withdrawal itself is reproduced widely.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GLUCOSE_DEPRIVATION" target="MTORC1" id="other:30b9314538d882f6">
  <data key="d9">GLUCOSE_DEPRIVATION</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Glucose withdrawal suppresses mTORC1 even in AMPK-null cells, so an AMPK-independent arm operates in parallel with the LKB1-AMPK route; the proposed effectors, Rag-GTPase-dependent release of mTORC1 from the lysosome and hexokinase-II binding to Raptor, each rest on a single report.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">155; 496</data>
  <data key="d17">AMPK-independent arm</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The existence of an AMPK-independent arm is well supported; the molecular identity of that arm is not.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GLUCOSE_DEPRIVATION" target="RAG_HETERODIMER" id="sensing:77c8ec4da4d5b965">
  <data key="d9">GLUCOSE_DEPRIVATION</data>
  <data key="d10">RAG_HETERODIMER</data>
  <data key="d11">sensing</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Glucose withdrawal shifts the Rag heterodimer to its inactive nucleotide state, releasing mTORC1 from the lysosomal surface; mice expressing GTP-locked RagA cannot execute this switch and die of neonatal glucose-starvation failure.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">155</data>
  <data key="d17">glucose deprivation</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The in vivo genetic evidence comes from mice expressing GTP-locked RagA, which cannot inactivate the heterodimer and die of neonatal starvation.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="GLYCOGEN" target="PRKAB1" id="binding:b7ed3b71bdc57417">
  <data key="d9">GLYCOGEN</data>
  <data key="d10">PRKAB1</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">The carbohydrate-binding module of the AMPK β-subunit binds glycogen, and branched glycogen with exposed α-1,6 branch points inhibits AMPK activity and Thr172 phosphorylation, so cellular carbohydrate reserves restrain the energy-stress arm independently of the AMP:ATP ratio.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">403</data>
  <data key="d17">muscle and liver glycogen stores</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Glycogen reserves restrain AMPK independently of the AMP:ATP ratio, which is why glycogen depletion is a determinant of AMPK activation during exercise.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HYPOXIA" target="AMPK" id="sensing:d700c193cc6e2d1d">
  <data key="d9">HYPOXIA</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">sensing</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Limited oxidative phosphorylation lowers ATP and raises AMP/ADP, activating LKB1-AMPK, which phosphorylates TSC2 and Raptor; a REDD1-independent arm of hypoxic mTORC1 inhibition.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">357</data>
  <data key="d17">hypoxia</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">the REDD1-TSC route acts without AMPK or LKB1. Mitochondrial ROS have also been proposed to activate AMPK in hypoxia independently of the AMP:ATP ratio.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HYPOXIA" target="DDIT4" id="transcriptional_regulation:4865493c168022dc">
  <data key="d9">HYPOXIA</data>
  <data key="d10">DDIT4</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Hypoxia induces REDD1 (via HIF-1 and, under severe hypoxia/ER stress, ATF4); REDD1 is required for hypoxic mTORC1 inhibition, which is lost in REDD1-/- and TSC2-/- cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">58; 139</data>
  <data key="d17">hypoxia</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HYPOXIA" target="EGLN1" id="other:71555f634fc53331">
  <data key="d9">HYPOXIA</data>
  <data key="d10">EGLN1</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Falling O2 tension limits the prolyl-hydroxylase reaction, inactivating PHD2 and allowing HIF-1α to escape hydroxylation and degradation; this is the molecular basis of the HIF-1α stabilisation that drives REDD1 and BNIP3 induction.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">162; 293</data>
  <data key="d17">hypoxia</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HYPOXIA" target="HIF1A" id="stabilisation:da946ad3e70d8a9d">
  <data key="d9">HYPOXIA</data>
  <data key="d10">HIF1A</data>
  <data key="d11">stabilisation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Low O2 blocks PHD-mediated prolyl hydroxylation of HIF-1alpha; VHL no longer binds, ubiquitination ceases and HIF-1alpha accumulates to drive hypoxia-responsive genes including DDIT4 and BNIP3.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Pro402; Pro564</data>
  <data key="d16">266; 268</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">HIF-1alpha is itself a translational output of mTORC1, so hypoxic signalling through HIF-1alpha closes a feedback loop onto the pathway.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="HYPOXIA" target="MTORC1" id="other:01a9b0f351730f85">
  <data key="d9">HYPOXIA</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Hypoxia suppresses mTORC1 by two indirect routes: HIF-1alpha-dependent induction of REDD1 with consequent TSC activation, which proceeds without AMPK or LKB1, and, when hypoxia is severe enough to lower ATP, activation of AMPK.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">58; 139; 357</data>
  <data key="d17">hypoxia</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">A summary of two indirect routes rather than a direct interaction.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
