  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="METFORMIN" target="RAG_HETERODIMER" id="other:0965b8c9a0a344de">
  <data key="d9">METFORMIN</data>
  <data key="d10">RAG_HETERODIMER</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Metformin inhibits mTORC1 independently of AMPK and TSC2 in a Rag GTPase-dependent manner; constitutively active Rag mutants blunt the effect, suggesting interference with Rag-mediated lysosomal recruitment.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">295</data>
  <data key="d17">AMPK-null or TSC2-null cells</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">the Rag step is inferred from constitutively active Rag mutants rather than demonstrated directly.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="METFORMIN" target="MTORC1" id="other:96334e3a9c66cf00">
  <data key="d9">METFORMIN</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Metformin lowers mTORC1 activity indirectly and does not bind mTOR. One route is AMPK-dependent, through phosphorylation of Raptor at Ser792 and enhancement of TSC2 GAP activity; a second is AMPK-independent, requires the Rag GTPases and was demonstrated in cells lacking AMPK or TSC2. Because of the second route, suppression of mTORC1 cannot be inferred from AMPK activation alone.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">172; 243; 295</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The AMPK-independent, Rag-dependent route is dose-dependent and rests on a smaller body of work than the AMPK-dependent route.</data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
