<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="CAP_DEPENDENT_TRANSLATION" id="drug_inhibition:6455d8a24b6b8557">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">CAP_DEPENDENT_TRANSLATION</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Rapalogs suppress cap-dependent translation only partially. With FKBP12-rapamycin bound to the FRB domain, phosphorylation of 4E-BP1 at Ser65 and Thr70 is blocked while Thr37 and Thr46 remain largely phosphorylated, so eIF4F assembly continues at a substantial rate. The limitation is structural rather than a matter of dose.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">40; 70; 166; 603</data>
  <data key="d17">rapalog exposure</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">ATP-competitive and bi-steric inhibitors suppress cap-dependent translation far more completely.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="EIF4EBP1" id="drug_inhibition:ab7b2d8963cd5d11">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Rapamycin blocks phosphorylation of the late 4E-BP1 sites Ser65 and Thr70 while largely sparing the priming sites Thr37 and Thr46, so binding of 4E-BP1 to eIF4E is only partly restored and cap-dependent translation continues. The limitation is structural rather than a matter of dose.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser65; Thr70</data>
  <data key="d16">40; 70; 111; 166; 603</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">The degree of resistance varies with cell type, and 4E-BP1 phosphorylation can recover during prolonged exposure.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="EIF4EBP1" id="drug_inhibition:f28da82e90ab7ad9">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">Thr37/Thr46 phosphorylation is relatively rapamycin-resistant in many experimental settings, limiting restoration of 4E-BP1-mediated repression of eIF4E.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr37; Thr46</data>
  <data key="d16">40; 70; 193; 603</data>
  <data key="d17">rapamycin-resistant sites</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Phosphosite sensitivity and the resulting translational response depend on cell type and exposure.</data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS" id="drug_inhibition:e05549f5d9a673f7">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Rapamycin lowers activating Ser44 phosphorylation of TIF-IA/RRN3 and raises the inhibitory Ser199 site, blocks the S6K1-dependent phosphorylation of the UBF carboxy-terminal activation domain, and relieves mTORC1 repression of MAF1 — collapsing Pol I 47S pre-rRNA synthesis and Pol III tRNA/5S output together.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">214; 255; 400; 401; 409; 410; 565</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">Rapamycin acts on both polymerase arms: phosphorylation of TIF-IA and of the UBF carboxy-terminal activation domain falls rapidly, while MAF1 escapes mTORC1-dependent inactivation and continues to repress RNA polymerase III.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="RPS6KB1" id="drug_inhibition:964e8ad5afbc9028">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">FKBP12-rapamycin bound to the mTORC1 FRB domain abolishes S6K1 Thr389 phosphorylation completely, in contrast to its partial effect on 4E-BP1. This makes Thr389 the pharmacodynamic marker of mTORC1 engagement and the readout against which partial pathway inhibition is judged in dosing studies.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr389</data>
  <data key="d16">111; 166; 603</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="MTORC2" id="drug_inhibition:b62e657543074ed2">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Prolonged rapamycin exposure inhibits mTORC2 because FKBP12–rapamycin captures newly synthesised mTOR before assembly; mTORC1 selectivity is kinetic, not absolute.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">527</data>
  <data key="d17">chronic or continuous dosing</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="MTORC2" id="drug_inhibition:aacf8eca6c992930">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Chronic dosing lowers mTORC2 output, including AKT Ser473, in many but not all cell types, by blocking assembly of new complexes; selectivity for mTORC1 is therefore kinetic rather than absolute.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">527</data>
  <data key="d17">chronic dosing; cell-type dependent</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">Cell-type variability in mTORC2 sensitivity is itself well replicated.</data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="PHENO_GLUCOSE_INTOLERANCE" id="other:d8ac084c97ba1163">
  <data key="d9">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_GLUCOSE_INTOLERANCE</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Chronic rapamycin exposure disrupts mTORC2 and causes insulin resistance, an effect uncoupled from its longevity benefit.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">325</data>
  <data key="d17">continuous high exposure in rodents</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">Whether intermittent dosing, which allows mTORC2 to recover between doses, spares glucose metabolism remains unsettled; the supporting evidence is preliminary [12].</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAPAMYCIN" target="PHENO_GLUCOSE_INTOLERANCE" id="drug_inhibition:736366dc82c08855">
