</edge>
<edge source="TORIN2" target="MTOR" id="competitive_inhibition:0bc29deeb3499ee5">
  <data key="d9">TORIN2</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">competitive_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Torin2 occupies the mTOR ATP-binding cleft and inhibits both complexes, including the rapamycin-resistant 4E-BP1 sites. It is more potent and more orally bioavailable than Torin1 but also inhibits the related PIKK family members ATM, ATR and DNA-PK, so selectivity for mTOR is incomplete at higher concentrations.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">166; 358; 603</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">Used as a laboratory tool compound rather than a clinical agent.</data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VAR_FRB_RESISTANCE" target="FRB_DOMAIN" id="genetic_loss_of_function:3453387e35fc20b2">
  <data key="d9">VAR_FRB_RESISTANCE</data>
  <data key="d10">FRB_DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d11">genetic_loss_of_function</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Substitution of the critical serine contact in the FRB domain of human mTOR, Ser2035, abolishes docking of the FKBP12-rapamycin complex while leaving kinase activity intact. FRB substitutions selected under prolonged rapamycin exposure, Ala2034Val and Phe2108Leu, confer rapalog resistance by the same route, whereas the kinase-domain substitution Met2327Ile confers resistance to ATP-competitive inhibitors without preventing drug docking.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser2035</data>
  <data key="d16">98; 365; 501</data>
  <data key="d17">acquired and engineered rapamycin resistance</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">Bivalent agents that engage the FRB and the ATP site together retain activity against these substitutions.</data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VISTUSERTIB" target="MTOR" id="competitive_inhibition:72cc908e959956b7">
  <data key="d9">VISTUSERTIB</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">competitive_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Vistusertib competes with ATP at the mTOR kinase cleft, inhibiting mTORC1 and mTORC2; it was selected from the AZD8055 series for improved pharmaceutical properties.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">112; 166; 473</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VISTUSERTIB" target="MTORC1" id="drug_inhibition:8e8ec25c3c999b7f">
  <data key="d9">VISTUSERTIB</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d11">drug_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Vistusertib occupies the mTOR ATP cleft, which is common to both complexes, and therefore suppresses mTORC1 output completely, including the 4E-BP1 phosphorylation sites that allosteric agents leave only partly suppressed.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">112; 166; 473</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
