<edge source="SLC3A2" target="SLC7A5" id="complex_formation:bac7ee7247f3febc">
  <data key="d9">SLC3A2</data>
  <data key="d10">SLC7A5</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">SLC3A2 (4F2hc/CD98 heavy chain) is disulphide-bonded to the SLC7A5 light chain; the heavy chain is required for surface delivery and transport function of the LAT1 heterodimer.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">396; 435</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The heavy chain is transport-inactive on its own and acts as the trafficking subunit; SLC7A5 carries the transport site.</data>
  <data key="d20">lysosomal_nutrient_sensing</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SLC7A5" target="GLUTAMINE" id="transport:7a176d935e1bf94d">
  <data key="d9">SLC7A5</data>
  <data key="d10">GLUTAMINE</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">LAT1/4F2hc is an obligatory antiporter: it effluxes intracellular glutamine in exchange for extracellular leucine, so SLC1A5-mediated glutamine uptake is the driving force for leucine import. This coupling is why glutamine deprivation blunts leucine-driven mTORC1 activation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">435</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Obligatory antiport: leucine import is coupled to glutamine efflux, so this route depletes rather than supplies the cytosolic glutamine pool, which is why glutamine deprivation blunts leucine-driven mTORC1 activation.</data>
  <data key="d20">lysosomal_nutrient_sensing</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="SLC7A5" target="LEUCINE" id="transport:6b89bf895cb6337f">
  <data key="d9">SLC7A5</data>
  <data key="d10">LEUCINE</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">LAT1 imports leucine at the plasma membrane in obligatory exchange for efflux of intracellular glutamine, setting the cytosolic leucine concentration seen by the sensors</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">435</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Leucine import through LAT1 is obligatorily coupled to efflux of intracellular glutamine.</data>
  <data key="d20">lysosomal_nutrient_sensing</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="V_ATPASE" target="AMINO_ACIDS" id="transport:def4b752f80fa45b">
  <data key="d9">V_ATPASE</data>
  <data key="d10">AMINO_ACIDS</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">The proton gradient generated by the v-ATPase drives amino-acid efflux from the lysosomal lumen; lysosomal metabolomics shows v-ATPase- and mTOR-dependent control of lysosomal amino-acid export, placing the pump upstream of SLC38A9-dependent efflux as well as of the inside-out Ragulator signal.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">1; 708</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Transport-level role of the v-ATPase, distinct from its inside-out signalling to Ragulator.</data>
  <data key="d20">lysosomal_nutrient_sensing</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
