</edge>
<edge source="ABCB1" target="PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE" id="transport:1f859310a83b1d07">
  <data key="d9">ABCB1</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Sirolimus and everolimus are ABCB1 substrates; brain accumulation of everolimus rises when the transporter is absent or pharmacologically inhibited.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">594</data>
  <data key="d17">blood-brain barrier</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">Shields CNS from systemic exposure but limits efficacy wherever CNS target engagement is the object.</data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ABCB1" target="PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE" id="transport:a41a437415b0a4f1">
  <data key="d9">ABCB1</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Efflux at the blood-brain barrier shields the CNS from systemic exposure but equally limits target engagement wherever the brain is the therapeutic object, decoupling central from peripheral exposure.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">594</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19">A dose titrated to a peripheral marker such as whole-blood S6K1 phosphorylation is not thereby titrated in brain.</data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ABCB1" target="RAPAMYCIN" id="transport:fd7def28ef5bfaea">
  <data key="d9">ABCB1</data>
  <data key="d10">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Sirolimus is an ABCB1 substrate, so central and peripheral exposure-response relationships cannot be assumed to coincide; a dose titrated to whole-blood S6K1 phosphorylation is not thereby titrated in brain.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">594</data>
  <data key="d17">blood-brain barrier</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ABCB1" target="RAPAMYCIN" id="transport:ac584fdc440c698e">
  <data key="d9">ABCB1</data>
  <data key="d10">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">P-glycoprotein effluxes sirolimus at the apical membrane of enterocytes, which together with first-pass CYP3A metabolism keeps oral bioavailability low: approximately 14% for the oral solution, with tablet exposure about 27% higher and not bioequivalent to it. The same transporter handling underlies the extensive drug-interaction profile of the drug.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">373</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ABCB1" target="TEMSIROLIMUS" id="transport:2e8709e9abee5c93">
  <data key="d9">ABCB1</data>
  <data key="d10">TEMSIROLIMUS</data>
  <data key="d11">transport</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Temsirolimus and the sirolimus it yields are P-glycoprotein substrates, so efflux limits their distribution into tissues, most consequentially across the blood-brain barrier. Because temsirolimus is administered intravenously, intestinal efflux does not contribute to its systemic exposure.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">373</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CYP3A4" target="EVEROLIMUS" id="metabolic_conversion:c444599cc1321974">
  <data key="d9">CYP3A4</data>
  <data key="d10">EVEROLIMUS</data>
  <data key="d11">metabolic_conversion</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Everolimus is likewise a CYP3A4 substrate, giving extensive interaction potential with azoles, macrolides, rifampicin and anticonvulsants and requiring exposure-guided dosing.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">314</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CYP3A4" target="PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE" id="metabolic_conversion:64b0ed304024f24c">
  <data key="d9">CYP3A4</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_CNS_DRUG_EXPOSURE</data>
  <data key="d11">metabolic_conversion</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">In mice, CYP3A acts together with plasma carboxylesterase to clear everolimus systemically and so sets the blood concentration from which central exposure derives; restriction of brain penetration itself is attributed chiefly to P-glycoprotein at the blood-brain barrier.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">594</data>
  <data key="d17">mouse disposition study</data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CYP3A4" target="RAPAMYCIN" id="metabolic_conversion:836c4dd3aa372865">
  <data key="d9">CYP3A4</data>
  <data key="d10">RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d11">metabolic_conversion</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">CYP3A4 in gut and liver demethylates and hydroxylates sirolimus, so CYP3A4 inducers and inhibitors shift its exposure several-fold; whole-blood trough monitoring, commonly in the range of 5-15 ng/mL in transplantation, is therefore standard.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">373</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CYP3A4" target="TEMSIROLIMUS" id="metabolic_conversion:b66b770de485d2c3">
  <data key="d9">CYP3A4</data>
  <data key="d10">TEMSIROLIMUS</data>
  <data key="d11">metabolic_conversion</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Temsirolimus is cleared principally by hepatic CYP3A4, which hydroxylates and demethylates it, so CYP3A4 inhibitors and inducers markedly alter its exposure. The drug is given intravenously, so no intestinal first-pass step contributes, and its principal active metabolite sirolimus arises by hydrolysis of the temsirolimus ester rather than by CYP3A4.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">373; 564</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FKBP12" target="FKBP12_RAPAMYCIN" id="complex_formation:3e21801e38c8d103">
  <data key="d9">FKBP12</data>
  <data key="d10">FKBP12_RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">FKBP12, a peptidyl-prolyl isomerase, is the intracellular receptor for rapamycin and contributes one half of the composite surface that binds FRB. Inhibition of mTOR is a gain of function of the drug-immunophilin complex, not a consequence of blocking isomerase activity.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">24; 37; 56; 107; 108; 229; 230; 514; 515</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FKBP12_RAPAMYCIN" target="FRB_DOMAIN" id="binding:05c1a8291e2b6c45">
  <data key="d9">FKBP12_RAPAMYCIN</data>
  <data key="d10">FRB_DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">The composite FKBP12-rapamycin surface wedges into the FRB domain adjacent to the catalytic cleft, narrowing substrate access; this gain-of-function mechanism explains why rapamycin is an allosteric, substrate-selective inhibitor rather than an ATP-competitive one.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">24; 56; 107; 108; 514; 515</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">pharmacological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
