<edge source="AKT" target="TSC2" id="phosphorylation:0d91776c6f4efdae">
  <data key="d9">AKT</data>
  <data key="d10">TSC2</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">AKT phosphorylates TSC2 at five sites, Ser939 and Thr1462 being dominant, creating 14-3-3 binding sites; the demonstrated consequence is release of the TSC complex from the lysosomal membrane where Rheb resides, so Rheb·GTP accumulates. Stable dissociation of TSC1 from TSC2 remains poorly supported.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser1130; Ser1132; Ser939; Ser981; Thr1462</data>
  <data key="d16">72; 260; 386</data>
  <data key="d17">growth-factor stimulation</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Part of the double-negative AKT -| TSC -| Rheb circuit. AKT phosphorylated only at Thr308 retains substantial activity toward TSC2 [387,388].</data>
  <data key="d20">growth_factor_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AKT" target="TSC2" id="phosphorylation:9f073520b0aa6780">
  <data key="d9">AKT</data>
  <data key="d10">TSC2</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">AKT phosphorylates and inactivates TSC2, permitting Rheb·GTP accumulation; this output is largely retained by Thr308-only AKT.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser939; Thr1462</data>
  <data key="d16">72; 260; 386</data>
  <data key="d17">retained without AKT Ser473</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">TSC2, like GSK3beta, is a Ser473-independent AKT substrate.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AKT" target="TSC_COMPLEX" id="membrane_targeting:3f3ba53426ec6b6b">
  <data key="d9">AKT</data>
  <data key="d10">TSC_COMPLEX</data>
  <data key="d11">membrane_targeting</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Insulin/AKT-dependent TSC2 phosphorylation releases the TSC complex from lysosomal membranes, physically separating the Rheb GAP from Rheb.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">72; 407</data>
  <data key="d17">insulin stimulation</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Conversely, amino-acid starvation recruits TSC2 to the lysosome via Rag GTPases (Demetriades 2014, PMID:24529380).</data>
  <data key="d20">growth_factor_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AKT" target="AMPK" id="phosphorylation:167374bd110f7c61">
  <data key="d9">AKT</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">AKT phosphorylates the AMPK alpha1 ST-loop at human Ser487, reducing subsequent Thr172 phosphorylation by upstream kinases.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser487 (human AMPK alpha1; Ser485 in rat)</data>
  <data key="d16">228; 240</data>
  <data key="d17">AMPK alpha1; growth-factor-stimulated cells</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Human alpha2 Ser491 is not assigned to AKT: Hawley et al. found alpha2 autophosphorylation at that site.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AKT" target="MAPKAP1" id="phosphorylation:c9e3b18f2c6758e2">
  <data key="d9">AKT</data>
  <data key="d10">MAPKAP1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">AKT phosphorylates mSin1 at Thr86, which one laboratory found to enhance mTORC2 activity toward AKT Ser473 and so create positive feedback. Another laboratory found that Thr86 and Thr398 phosphorylation, triggered by S6K or AKT depending on cell context, dissociates mSin1 from mTORC2 and suppresses its activity.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr86</data>
  <data key="d16">252; 359; 671</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">The sign of Thr86 phosphorylation is unresolved between the two laboratories. The 2015 study identified AKT, not S6K, as the main Thr86 kinase in every cell line tested.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AKT" target="MTOR" id="phosphorylation:2bac0b25de41900b">
  <data key="d9">AKT</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
