<edge source="AMPK" target="TP53" id="phosphorylation:d2e14d0f0bbcab05">
  <data key="d9">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d10">TP53</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Glucose deprivation-activated AMPK phosphorylates p53 Ser15, initiating a p53-dependent metabolic checkpoint (cell-cycle arrest) and transcription of sestrins, REDD1, PTEN and TSC2 that feed back on mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser15</data>
  <data key="d16">282</data>
  <data key="d17">glucose deprivation</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AMPK" target="TSC2" id="phosphorylation:1a24c98098a1dca6">
  <data key="d9">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d10">TSC2</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Under energy stress AMPK phosphorylates TSC2 at Thr1271 and Ser1387, enhancing its GAP activity toward Rheb and lowering Rheb-GTP; this is required for energy-stress inhibition of mTORC1 and protects cells from apoptosis during energy deprivation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser1387; Thr1271</data>
  <data key="d16">187; 207; 216; 226; 262</data>
  <data key="d17">energy stress / glucose deprivation</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Standard human TSC2 numbering (1807 residues); Inoki et al. 2003 [262] reported these residues as Thr1227 and Ser1345. AMPK phosphorylation at Ser1387 primes GSK3 phosphorylation at Ser1383 and Ser1379 (Ser1341 and Ser1337 in the Inoki numbering).</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AMPK" target="ULK1" id="binding:1d7c9b1d8b1d2ef0">
  <data key="d9">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d10">ULK1</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">AMPK associates with ULK1 in a nutrient-dependent manner; the association is required for AMPK-mediated activating phosphorylation of ULK1 and is disrupted when mTORC1 phosphorylates ULK1 at Ser757 (mouse numbering; Ser758 in human ULK1).</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">157; 309</data>
  <data key="d17">energy stress / autophagy initiation</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Phosphorylation at Ser757 acts by disrupting the ULK1-AMPK interaction.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AMPK" target="ULK1" id="phosphorylation:3fd38186ffdf7df9">
  <data key="d9">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d10">ULK1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Under glucose starvation AMPK binds and phosphorylates ULK1 at multiple sites, activating it to initiate autophagy/mitophagy; this is prevented when mTORC1 phosphorylates ULK1 Ser757.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser317; Ser555; Ser777</data>
  <data key="d16">157; 309</data>
  <data key="d17">glucose starvation</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Ser317/Ser777 from Kim 2011 [309]; Egan 2011 [157] additionally mapped Ser467, Ser555, Thr574 and Ser637.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AMPK" target="ULK1" id="phosphorylation:78df1c518e4278b6">
  <data key="d9">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d10">ULK1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Energy-stress-activated AMPK phosphorylates ULK1 at activating sites, initiating autophagy and mitophagy; blocked when mTORC1 has marked Ser757</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser317; Ser555; Ser777</data>
  <data key="d16">157; 309</data>
  <data key="d17">mouse ULK1 site numbering; energy-stress models</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Mouse Ser555 corresponds to human Ser556; mouse Ser777 is not conserved in human ULK1. The effect of AMPK on autophagy depends on stress and timing.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AMPK" target="CRY1" id="phosphorylation:59cb3cde5c55444a">
  <data key="d9">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d10">CRY1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">AMPK phosphorylates CRY1, promoting FBXL3-dependent ubiquitination and degradation; this destabilises the clock's negative limb and entrains the oscillator to cellular energy state.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser71</data>
  <data key="d16">324</data>
  <data key="d17">mouse liver and fibroblasts; energy stress</data>
  <data key="d18">circadian</data>
  <data key="d19">Lamia 2009 reported Ser71 as the destabilising site, with a second site also described. PMID 19833968 (Science 2009;326(5951):437-440).</data>
  <data key="d20">circadian</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="AMPK" target="CSNK1E" id="phosphorylation:35c1618f3871c84b">
  <data key="d9">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d10">CSNK1E</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">AMPK phosphorylates casein kinase 1ε at Ser389, increasing its activity toward PER2; a second route (besides CRY1) by which cellular energy status reaches the clock</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser389</data>
  <data key="d16">617</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">circadian</data>
  <data key="d19">External identifier requires independent verification.</data>
  <data key="d20">circadian</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ATM" target="AMPK" id="other:5180799cb6f74391">
  <data key="d9">ATM</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Indirect relay: ROS-activated cytoplasmic ATM acts through LKB1 to increase AMPK Thr172 phosphorylation, and AMPK then phosphorylates TSC2, represses mTORC1 and induces autophagy; the response is lost in ATM-deficient cells, and no direct phosphorylation of AMPK by ATM has been shown.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">5</data>
  <data key="d17">oxidative stress (H2O2)</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Reported by one laboratory; the proposed ATM-LKB1 step is phosphorylation of LKB1 at Thr366.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ATM" target="STK11" id="phosphorylation:002477756004e15c">
  <data key="d9">ATM</data>
  <data key="d10">STK11</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">ATM phosphorylates LKB1 at Thr366 (Thr363 in mouse) in response to ionising radiation and oxidative stress; the modification does not change LKB1 catalytic activity in vitro, but a non-phosphorylatable LKB1 mutant failed to restore ROS-induced mTORC1 repression in LKB1-deficient cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr366</data>
  <data key="d16">5; 524</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage; oxidative stress</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Two laboratories have reported ATM phosphorylation of Thr366, but its requirement for mTORC1 repression rests on a single study.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ATM" target="TP53" id="phosphorylation:8a7b982896edee0b">
  <data key="d9">ATM</data>
  <data key="d10">TP53</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">ATM phosphorylates p53 Ser15 (and CHK2, which phosphorylates Ser20), weakening MDM2 binding and stabilising/activating p53 after DNA damage.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser15</data>
  <data key="d16">25; 76</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="BNIP3" target="RHEB" id="binding:2aed86a859ab7328">
  <data key="d9">BNIP3</data>
  <data key="d10">RHEB</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Hypoxia-induced BNIP3 binds Rheb directly and lowers Rheb-GTP levels in a manner requiring its N-terminal domain, contributing to mTORC1 inhibition in hypoxia.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">347</data>
  <data key="d17">hypoxia</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Reported by one group; mechanism of Rheb nucleotide change not fully defined.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="DDIT4" target="TSC2" id="other:74e5d6bc227bb5b7">
  <data key="d9">DDIT4</data>
  <data key="d10">TSC2</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">REDD1-dependent mTORC1 inhibition requires TSC2 in the cited models. This functional relationship does not establish direct REDD1 binding to 14-3-3 or direct stimulation of TSC2 catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">58; 139; 626</data>
  <data key="d17">hypoxia, energy and ER stress</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="DDIT4" target="YWHA" id="binding:a4dc1c8aabb6d75f">
  <data key="d9">DDIT4</data>
  <data key="d10">YWHA</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Direct REDD1 binding to 14-3-3 and displacement of TSC2 were proposed, but a subsequent structural and biochemical study did not detect REDD1-14-3-3 binding.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">139; 626</data>
  <data key="d17">hypoxia</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The direct binding mechanism is disputed; this does not negate REDD1-dependent suppression of mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF2S1" target="ATF4" id="translational_regulation:c18234e90b6d9daf">
  <data key="d9">EIF2S1</data>
  <data key="d10">ATF4</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Phospho-eIF2alpha lowers ternary-complex availability; delayed reinitiation after uORF1 bypasses the inhibitory uORF2 so the ATF4 coding sequence is translated (integrated stress response).</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">219; 625</data>
  <data key="d17">integrated stress response</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Phosphorylation of eIF2alpha (an inhibitory event for eIF2) paradoxically increases ATF4 output.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PPM1A" target="AMPK" id="dephosphorylation:635282cf4a4ecf03">
  <data key="d9">PPM1A</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">dephosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">PP2C-family phosphatases dephosphorylate AMPK Thr172 and switch the kinase off; AMP, and roughly ten-fold less potently ADP, binds the gamma-subunit CBS sites and protects Thr172 from dephosphorylation. This protection is distinct from the direct allosteric activation of the already phosphorylated kinase and is the main route by which a falling energy charge sustains AMPK activity.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr172</data>
  <data key="d16">130; 200</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Graded emerging because protection from dephosphorylation is established for the PP2C class while the physiological phosphatase isoform (PPM1A, PPM1B, PPM1E or PPM1F) remains unresolved.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PRKAB1" target="AMPK" id="complex_formation:3c2a80a70fbd5e5b">
  <data key="d9">PRKAB1</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">Scaffolding beta subunit (beta1 or beta2) bridging alpha and gamma; its C-terminus binds both, its CBM binds glycogen and contributes to the ADaM drug site.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">187; 216</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PRKAG1" target="AMPK" id="complex_formation:74f04c5f3ed3ffa8">
  <data key="d9">PRKAG1</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">Regulatory gamma subunit (gamma1/2/3) whose four tandem CBS motifs form two Bateman domains with three occupied adenine-nucleotide sites: one holds AMP almost permanently, two exchange AMP, ADP and ATP, and one site remains unoccupied, conferring energy sensing on the heterotrimer.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">187; 216; 665</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TP53" target="DDIT4" id="transcriptional_regulation:526c04566b9b633e">
  <data key="d9">TP53</data>
  <data key="d10">DDIT4</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">p53 (and p63) bind the DDIT4/REDD1 promoter and induce it after DNA damage; REDD1 then releases TSC2 from 14-3-3 to suppress mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">160</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TP53" target="PRKAB1" id="transcriptional_regulation:1180b2372e3a2d13">
  <data key="d9">TP53</data>
  <data key="d10">PRKAB1</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">p53 transcriptionally induces the AMPK β1 regulatory subunit, raising AMPK heterotrimer abundance as part of the same transcriptional programme that induces sestrins, TSC2 and PTEN.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">169</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The AMPK-subunit arm of the p53 transcriptional programme; induction is stress-, cell-type- and tissue-specific rather than general.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TP53" target="PTEN" id="transcriptional_regulation:cb05f78e6d66ac6e">
  <data key="d9">TP53</data>
  <data key="d10">PTEN</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">p53 binds a PTEN promoter element and induces PTEN; higher PTEN lowers PIP3-AKT, relieving AKT inhibition of TSC2 and reducing mTORC1 as one arm of the coordinated p53-mTOR response.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">168; 574</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TP53" target="SESN1" id="transcriptional_regulation:aac251f0be0dd853">
  <data key="d9">TP53</data>
  <data key="d10">SESN1</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">p53 binds response elements in SESN1 (PA26) and induces it after DNA damage; Sestrin1 contributes to p53-dependent mTORC1 suppression via GATOR2 binding and AMPK.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">65; 628</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">SESN1 (PA26) carries a functional p53-responsive element and is induced by genotoxic stress in a p53-dependent manner.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TP53" target="SESN2" id="transcriptional_regulation:59ab55f483216c95">
  <data key="d9">TP53</data>
  <data key="d10">SESN2</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">p53 induces SESN2 (Hi95) after genotoxic stress; Sestrin2 then inhibits mTORC1, principally by sequestering GATOR2, linking DNA damage to growth arrest.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">64; 65</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Induction by DNA damage is p53-dependent, whereas induction by prolonged hypoxia or oxidative stress is largely p53-independent.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="TP53" target="TSC2" id="transcriptional_regulation:de78600f7a3510bf">
  <data key="d9">TP53</data>
  <data key="d10">TSC2</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">p53 transcriptionally induces TSC2 in a stress-, cell-type- and tissue-specific manner, raising Rheb-GAP capacity and reinforcing mTORC1 suppression after genotoxic stress.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">168; 169</data>
  <data key="d17">DNA damage; mouse tissues and cell lines</data>
  <data key="d18">upstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Induction is conditional rather than general; the dominant p53 route to mTORC1 runs through sestrins and AMPK. Induction of the AMPK beta1 subunit is documented in the same studies.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VHL" target="HIF1A" id="ubiquitination:f83283a4a48bde37">
  <data key="d9">VHL</data>
  <data key="d10">HIF1A</data>
  <data key="d11">ubiquitination</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">pVHL, the substrate-recognition subunit of a CUL2–elongin BC E3 ligase, binds prolyl-hydroxylated HIF-1α and polyubiquitinates it for proteasomal degradation; VHL loss in clear-cell renal carcinoma produces constitutive HIF signalling.</data>
