<edge source="ATP_SITE" target="MTOR" id="other:e7288a7fb751d099">
  <data key="d9">ATP_SITE</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">The ATP-binding cleft lies in the bilobed kinase domain of mTOR and is identical in mTORC1 and mTORC2, which is why agents that occupy it inhibit both complexes, unlike allosteric agents acting at the FRB domain.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">315; 588; 670</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">pharmacology</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CRIM_DOMAIN" target="AKT" id="recruitment:5dd9431fc4de3cad">
  <data key="d9">CRIM_DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d10">AKT</data>
  <data key="d11">recruitment</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">The mSin1 CRIM domain is a ubiquitin-like fold whose acidic loop binds AGC-kinase substrates at a site distal to the catalytic cleft; the basic surface of the AKT kinase N-lobe engages this loop about 75 Å from the mTOR active site. CRIM-dependent recruitment also applies to SGK and PKC, although different AGC kinases differ in their sensitivity to perturbation of mSin1.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">74; 483; 596; 597</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC2</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="CRIM_DOMAIN" target="MAPKAP1" id="binding:a1d95725b4993221">
  <data key="d9">CRIM_DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d10">MAPKAP1</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">The CRIM domain is the central substrate-recruitment module of mSin1.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">74; 483</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ETV7" target="MTOR" id="binding:53a511b3f5b8c81a">
  <data key="d9">ETV7</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">ETV7 is reported to interact with mTOR in the cytoplasm and to assemble a putative third complex, mTORC3, that lacks core mTORC1 and mTORC2 subunits and keeps mTOR activity in the presence of rapamycin. Tumour cells that lose this complex become rapamycin-sensitive.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">225</data>
  <data key="d17">ETV7-expressing tumour cells</data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
  <data key="d19">Reported by a single laboratory and not widely replicated; mTORC3 remains an emerging concept.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ETV7" target="MTORC3" id="complex_formation:a2fb973a850922c4">
  <data key="d9">ETV7</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC3</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">The ETS repressor ETV7 is reported to nucleate the putative third complex and to confer rapamycin insensitivity on it.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">225</data>
  <data key="d17">cancer cell lines</data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
  <data key="d19">Not independently replicated.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="ETV7" target="MTORC3" id="complex_formation:e0824cdae5df34a3">
  <data key="d9">ETV7</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC3</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">ETV7 is reported as the defining subunit of a putative third mTOR assembly, designated mTORC3, which is rapamycin-insensitive and supports proliferation in cancer cells.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">225</data>
  <data key="d17">cancer cell lines</data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
  <data key="d19">A contested claim: single-laboratory description, not widely replicated, and the supporting evidence is weak.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="FATC_DOMAIN" target="KINASE_DOMAIN" id="binding:3d9bfbef0acdf4ad">
  <data key="d9">FATC_DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d10">KINASE_DOMAIN</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">The C-terminal FATC motif is integral to the kinase domain and packs against the activation loop and the mLST8-binding element. Deleting the conserved C-terminal residues, or substituting a conserved tryptophan within them, nearly abolishes mTOR kinase activity.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">315; 588; 590; 670</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
