<edge source="MTOR" target="PHENO_OSTEOCLAST_DIFFERENTIATION" id="physiological_output:57487ccae9512b2e">
  <data key="d9">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_OSTEOCLAST_DIFFERENTIATION</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">The role of mTOR in osteoclast differentiation is unsettled: rapamycin suppresses RANKL-driven osteoclastogenesis in culture, whereas deletion of Raptor in osteoclast precursors has been reported to enhance differentiation and low-dose rapamycin can increase bone resorption in vivo. The net skeletal effect of inhibition is therefore context-dependent and not predictable from cell-autonomous data.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">101; 495</data>
  <data key="d17">bone</data>
  <data key="d18">pathophysiological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
