  <data key="d19">Site numbering follows the phosphoproteomic reports; the stabilisation, not the exact residues, is the established claim.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="GRB10" id="stabilisation:53d794b49d748b85">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">GRB10</data>
  <data key="d11">stabilisation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">mTORC1-dependent phosphorylation of Grb10 stabilises the protein against degradation and raises its steady-state level; stabilised Grb10 binds and desensitises the insulin and IGF-1 receptors, forming one of the negative-feedback brakes on upstream signalling.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">248; 687</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="AMPK" id="phosphorylation:ab42512ff7651fe1">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">AMPK</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">mTORC1 phosphorylates AMPK alpha2 Ser345 (Raptor-dependent) under nutrient stress, suppressing AMPK activity and promoting proliferation: a reciprocal brake opposing AMPK inhibition of mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser345</data>
  <data key="d16">353</data>
  <data key="d17">nutrient stress; proliferating cells</data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Single-laboratory finding awaiting independent replication; a reciprocal brake opposing AMPK inhibition of mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="ULK1" id="phosphorylation:3792b522d165903f">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">ULK1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">In nutrient sufficiency mTORC1 phosphorylates ULK1 at Ser757 (mouse numbering; human Ser758), disrupting the ULK1-AMPK interaction and preventing AMPK-mediated activating phosphorylation at Ser317, Ser555 and Ser777; AMPK and mTORC1 therefore toggle autophagy initiation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser757</data>
  <data key="d16">157; 183; 242; 286; 309</data>
  <data key="d17">nutrient sufficiency; mouse site numbering</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">energy_stress_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="ULK1" id="phosphorylation:2e2e4d2490e7594a">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">ULK1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">ULK1 Ser637 (mouse numbering; human Ser638) is dephosphorylated during nutrient starvation and its phosphorylation falls with mTOR inhibition, but the same site is also assigned to AMPK, so the responsible kinase is unresolved.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser637</data>
  <data key="d16">550</data>
  <data key="d17">mouse site numbering; human experiments in Shang 2011</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The kinase assignment for this site conflicts between studies, and the consequences for ULK1-AMPK binding differ between experimental systems.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="DEPTOR" id="phosphorylation:c1c471c24452c553">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">DEPTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Active mTOR phosphorylates DEPTOR in the linker between its DEP and PDZ domains. This primes the degron for CK1alpha and marks DEPTOR for betaTrCP-dependent degradation, so active mTOR removes its own brake.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">150; 186; 466</data>
  <data key="d17">growth-factor/nutrient sufficiency</data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Zhao et al. 2011 attributed degron phosphorylation to S6K1 and RSK1 rather than to mTOR directly.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="CAP_DEPENDENT_TRANSLATION" id="translational_regulation:88fdffaf4829f9f1">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">CAP_DEPENDENT_TRANSLATION</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Through 4E-BP phosphorylation and the S6K1-eIF4B/PDCD4/eEF2K axis, mTORC1 stimulates cap-dependent initiation and elongation. The effect is selective rather than global, and the most mTORC1-sensitive mRNAs, mainly TOP and TOP-like transcripts, depend chiefly on the 4E-BPs.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">40; 109; 193; 246; 453; 572; 604</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="EEF2K" id="phosphorylation:710df05fc4000e32">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">EEF2K</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Rapamycin-sensitive phosphorylation of eEF2K at Ser78 (adjacent to the calmodulin-binding site) impairs calmodulin binding and eEF2K activity; direct kinase not definitively identified.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser78</data>
  <data key="d16">57</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Site is mTOR-dependent; whether mTORC1 phosphorylates it directly is unresolved.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="EIF3" id="recruitment:4a22059a9859b72b">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF3</data>
  <data key="d11">recruitment</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Growth factors and nutrients recruit mTOR-Raptor to the eIF3 preinitiation complex, where mTORC1 phosphorylates S6K1 and 4E-BP1 through dynamic protein interchange.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">236</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="EIF4EBP1" id="phosphorylation:cc04c4fdce0461c9">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">mTORC1 first phosphorylates priming sites Thr37/Thr46; these are largely rapamycin-resistant, which is the structural reason rapalogs incompletely suppress cap-dependent translation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr37; Thr46</data>
  <data key="d16">40; 68; 70; 109; 193; 194; 453</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Priming step of the hierarchical mechanism.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="EIF4EBP1" id="phosphorylation:d15451868b587875">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">After Thr37/46 priming, mTORC1 phosphorylates Thr70 then Ser65 in a TOS-dependent, rapamycin-sensitive manner, lowering eIF4E affinity by orders of magnitude and releasing eIF4E.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
