<edge source="MTORC1" target="TISSUE_HAEMATOPOIETIC" id="physiological_output:1bc647a4751259c4">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_HAEMATOPOIETIC</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Proliferating haematopoietic progenitors require mTORC1, which accounts for the cytopenias seen during rapalog therapy. Restraint of mTORC1 by the TSC complex is conversely required to keep haematopoietic stem cells quiescent: Tsc1 deletion drives stem cells out of quiescence, increases mitochondrial biogenesis and reactive oxygen species, and impairs self-renewal, so the compartment requires calibrated rather than maximal activity.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">99</data>
  <data key="d17">bone marrow</data>
  <data key="d18">pathophysiological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="TISSUE_HEART" id="physiological_output:3b0d9ef16c12bbac">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_HEART</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Cardiac mTORC1 supports physiological hypertrophy; only the mTORC2 arm of the cardiomyocyte row was encoded.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">405; 538</data>
  <data key="d17">cardiomyocyte</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="TISSUE_HYPOTHALAMUS" id="physiological_output:ac4a5c95844f9dbb">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_HYPOTHALAMUS</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">In the arcuate nucleus mTORC1 operates as a nutrient sensor, which makes the hyperphagia of Smith-Kingsmore syndrome mechanistically expected rather than incidental.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">120; 419</data>
  <data key="d17">hypothalamic arcuate nucleus, rodent</data>
  <data key="d18">pathophysiological</data>
  <data key="d19">Established in rodents and unresolved in humans, where the central and peripheral contributions have not been separated; some metabolic effects attributed to liver, adipose tissue and beta-cells may therefore be centrally mediated.</data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="TISSUE_KIDNEY_PODOCYTE" id="physiological_output:e2d33bae09e5f771">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_KIDNEY_PODOCYTE</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Podocyte Raptor deletion produces injury and proteinuria while Tsc1 deletion causes glomerulosclerosis: a calibrated rather than monotonic requirement.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">208; 264</data>
  <data key="d17">podocyte</data>
  <data key="d18">pathophysiological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="TISSUE_LIVER" id="physiological_output:9bdcf45153eb2d2d">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_LIVER</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Hepatic mTORC1 drives insulin-stimulated SREBP1c-dependent lipogenesis and restrains fasting ketogenesis.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">212; 326; 677</data>
  <data key="d17">liver</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Activation of hepatic mTORC1 alone is not sufficient to drive lipogenesis: liver Tsc1 deletion lowers Akt signalling and protects against diet-induced steatosis, so Akt-dependent input is required alongside mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC1" target="TISSUE_MICROGLIA" id="physiological_output:dfa015229b595db2">
  <data key="d9">MTORC1</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_MICROGLIA</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">Pathway hyperactivation in the microglial compartment drives astrocyte proliferation and seizures, a non-neuronal contribution to the mTORopathies.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">702</data>
  <data key="d17">microglia</data>
  <data key="d18">pathophysiological</data>
