  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">The intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity of mTOR within mTORC2 has been reported to phosphorylate and activate the type I IGF receptor and the insulin receptor, constituting a feed-forward arm from mTORC2 back onto the receptor tier that would amplify PI3K-PIP3 input to mTORC2 itself.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15">receptor tyrosine residues</data>
  <data key="d16">678</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Graded emerging: the claim that mTOR has tyrosine kinase activity is not independently replicated as widely as the serine/threonine activity and should not be presented as settled.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC2" target="INSULIN_SECRETION" id="physiological_output:829283b5119f9174">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">INSULIN_SECRETION</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Insulin secretion and beta-cell survival depend on mTORC2 in the islet, so both complexes are required there, unlike the opposing arrangement in liver and adipose tissue; reduced insulin output contributes to the dysglycaemia seen at immunosuppressive exposures of mTOR inhibitors.</data>
