<edge source="MTORC2" target="MTOR" id="phosphorylation:d60a01a2543e21fd">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">mTOR autophosphorylates at Ser2481 within the assembled complex; in mTORC2 this autophosphorylation is a complex-specific readout of intrinsic catalytic activity and is rapamycin-insensitive under acute treatment, unlike the mTORC1-associated pool.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser2481</data>
  <data key="d16">571</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
  <data key="d19">Ser2481 is an activity-associated readout; the cited study does not establish that phosphorylation at this site activates mTOR.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC2" target="MYELINATION" id="physiological_output:8307fed79a2a1495">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">MYELINATION</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">Oligodendrocyte-lineage Rictor deletion has modest and largely transient effects on myelination, whereas Raptor loss causes persistent hypomyelination; only the raptor/rictor double mutant impairs oligodendrocyte differentiation itself, indicating that mTORC2 contributes but is not rate-limiting while mTORC1 remains intact.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">39; 332</data>
  <data key="d17">rodent; regional - spinal cord affected more than corpus callosum</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC2</data>
  <data key="d19">Effect is 'modulates', not 'required_for': the cited work shows mTORC2 is explicitly NOT rate-limiting. Encoding this as a requirement would misrepresent the source.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC2" target="PHENO_AGE_MEMORY_IMPAIRMENT" id="physiological_output:ccada256d0371f8a">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_AGE_MEMORY_IMPAIRMENT</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">A small molecule that raises mTORC2 activity reverses age-associated long-term memory impairment in aged mice and flies, and enhancing mTORC2-dependent actin polymerisation likewise improves long-term memory.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">280</data>
  <data key="d17">mouse and Drosophila</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC2</data>
  <data key="d19">Pharmacological gain-of-function evidence from a single report in mice and flies, with no independent replication and no human data.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC2" target="PHENO_BODY_GROWTH" id="physiological_output:36202feca90e5381">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_BODY_GROWTH</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">mTORC2 in adipose tissue negatively controls whole-body growth, so adipocyte Rictor deletion increases body size - a direction of effect opposite to the usual anabolic framing of the pathway.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">126</data>
  <data key="d17">adipose tissue</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC2</data>
  <data key="d19">Ref [126] (Cybulski 2009) is titled 'mTOR complex 2 in adipose tissue negatively controls whole-body growth'.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
