<edge source="MTORC2" target="PHENO_LONG_TERM_MEMORY" id="physiological_output:478b817c944741eb">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_LONG_TERM_MEMORY</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Conditional Rictor deletion in the postnatal murine forebrain selectively impairs long-term memory while sparing short-term memory; conversely, a compound that raises mTORC2 activity converts early-phase into late-phase potentiation, enhances long-term memory in wild-type animals and reverses age-associated memory impairment in mice and flies.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">250; 280</data>
  <data key="d17">rodent and Drosophila</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC2</data>
  <data key="d19">The requirement is bidirectional and pharmacologically accessible, which is what underlies concern about cognitive effects of inhibitors that reach mTORC2.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
