<edge source="MTORC2" target="TISSUE_CNS_NEURON" id="physiological_output:c2864eecc11f6536">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_CNS_NEURON</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Conditional deletion of Rictor in the postnatal murine forebrain reduces mTORC2 activity and selectively impairs late-phase long-term potentiation and long-term memory while sparing early-phase potentiation and short-term memory; the mechanism is Tiam1–Rac1-dependent actin polymerisation with reduced dendritic spine density, and raising mTORC2 activity pharmacologically enhances long-term memory and reverses its age-associated decline.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">250; 280</data>
  <data key="d17">CNS</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC2</data>
  <data key="d19">The rodent genetics are well supported; the inference that this creates a cognitive deficit missed by standard metabolic monitoring, and its extrapolation to chronic low-dose human dosing, are not.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MTORC2" target="TISSUE_HEART" id="physiological_output:0a95febc605a37ed">
  <data key="d9">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d10">TISSUE_HEART</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Cardiomyocyte mTORC2 limits apoptosis and tissue loss after ischaemic injury through AKT-dependent anti-apoptotic signalling, complementing the mTORC1 contribution to physiological growth, so agents that ablate both complexes are predicted to be more cardiotoxic than mTORC1-selective ones.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">631</data>
  <data key="d17">cardiomyocyte</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC2</data>
  <data key="d19">Shown in a myocardial infarction model in which suppression of mTORC2 increased cardiomyocyte apoptosis and tissue damage.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
