<edge source="RAIP_MOTIF" target="EIF4EBP1" id="binding:02ea409619ea73a7">
  <data key="d9">RAIP_MOTIF</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BP1 contains an N-terminal RAIP motif (Arg-Ala-Ile-Pro) that, together with the C-terminal TOS motif, is required for efficient mTORC1-dependent multisite phosphorylation; NMR places its contact on Raptor at a site separate from the TOS pocket.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">70; 109; 599</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Whether the RAIP motif contacts Raptor directly is disputed: Beugnet et al. 2003 (J Biol Chem 278:40717-40722) found that the RAIP-containing N-terminal fragment of 4E-BP1 does not bind Raptor.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RAIP_MOTIF" target="RPTOR" id="binding:dc84e9c6f1b8a21d">
  <data key="d9">RAIP_MOTIF</data>
  <data key="d10">RPTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">NMR shows that the N-terminal RAIP motif of 4E-BP1 binds Raptor at a site separate from the TOS-binding pocket, so that the two motifs tether 4E-BP1 by avidity and orient its phosphoacceptor region toward the kinase site; mutation of either motif abolishes Raptor co-immunoprecipitation of 4E-BP1.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">70; 109</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Beugnet et al. 2003 (J Biol Chem 278:40717-40722) found no Raptor binding by the RAIP-containing N-terminal fragment and concluded that RAIP promotes phosphorylation independently of Raptor binding; a direct RAIP-mTOR contact has not been demonstrated.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
