<edge source="MCOLN1" target="LYSOSOME" id="complex_formation:dc836d4e2d7f725a">
  <data key="d9">MCOLN1</data>
  <data key="d10">LYSOSOME</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">TRPML1 is a channel resident in the lysosomal membrane, placing the Ca2+-calcineurin-TFEB step on the lysosomal surface</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">406</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MAPKAP1_ISOFORM5" target="MTORC2" id="complex_formation:3672a04c5f3a8cba">
  <data key="d9">MAPKAP1_ISOFORM5</data>
  <data key="d10">MTORC2</data>
  <data key="d11">complex_formation</data>
  <data key="d12">component_of</data>
  <data key="d13">mSin1 splice isoforms assemble distinct mTORC2 species; the isoform lacking the C-terminal PH domain yields a complex not gated by PIP3.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">177</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">core</data>
  <data key="d19">Alternative splicing of mSin1 generates three mTORC2 species; the assignment comes from a single biochemical study and has not been independently reproduced, although the behaviour of the complex built on the isoform lacking the C-terminal PH domain is consistent with later work showing that the PH domain autoinhibits the complex until PIP3 binds.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VAR_MTOR_FAT_GOF" target="DISEASE_RCC" id="genetic_gain_of_function:81163fc5a8357c78">
  <data key="d9">VAR_MTOR_FAT_GOF</data>
  <data key="d10">DISEASE_RCC</data>
  <data key="d11">genetic_gain_of_function</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Activating MTOR mutations are increasingly recognised in renal cell carcinoma, clustering in FAT and kinase domains.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">201</data>
  <data key="d17">somatic, renal cell carcinoma</data>
  <data key="d18">pathophysiological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="VAR_MTOR_FAT_GOF" target="MTOR" id="genetic_gain_of_function:d66b02fe222cf3c1">
  <data key="d9">VAR_MTOR_FAT_GOF</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">genetic_gain_of_function</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Cancer-associated FAT-domain MTOR substitutions can increase mTOR signalling; the magnitude and substrate profile vary among variants.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">A1459P; C1483Y; E1799K</data>
  <data key="d16">201</data>
  <data key="d17">cancer-associated variants; biochemical and cell models</data>
  <data key="d18">pathophysiological</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
