  <data key="d9">BTRC</data>
  <data key="d10">PDCD4</data>
  <data key="d11">ubiquitination</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">SCF-betaTrCP recognises the phospho-Ser67 degron of PDCD4 and ubiquitinates it for proteasomal degradation after growth-factor stimulation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">147</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="EIF4E" id="sequestration:f578e663d7a352cb">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4E</data>
  <data key="d11">sequestration</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Hypophosphorylated 4E-BP1 binds the dorsal surface of eIF4E through the canonical YXXXXLΦ motif shared with eIF4G and through a secondary site, sequestering eIF4E from eIF4F. Multisite phosphorylation releases eIF4E; in the paralogue 4E-BP2, phosphorylation of Thr37 and Thr46 folds the protein into a β-domain that buries this motif.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">19; 40; 109; 193; 209; 453; 463; 572</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="EIF4F" id="competitive_inhibition:37d420f781e5eada">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4F</data>
  <data key="d11">competitive_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BP1 and eIF4G share the eIF4E-binding motif, so 4E-BP1 binding to eIF4E is mutually exclusive with eIF4G and blocks eIF4F assembly on the cap.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">374; 389; 572</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="PHENO_CELL_PROLIFERATION" id="physiological_output:2f377e919e432c4b">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_CELL_PROLIFERATION</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BPs mediate mTORC1 control of proliferation but not cell size: 4E-BP1/2-null cells are resistant to the antiproliferative effect of mTORC1 inhibition.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">148</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="TOP_MRNA" id="translational_regulation:4a4c0cd1c4f8130c">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">TOP_MRNA</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Ribosome profiling attributes TOP mRNA sensitivity to mTORC1 largely to 4E-BPs, whereas LARP1 studies show 4E-BP-independent repression; both arms likely contribute.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">470; 604</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Relative contribution of 4E-BP versus LARP1 to TOP repression remains debated.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="HIF1A" id="translational_regulation:58179a9389220457">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">HIF1A</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Hypophosphorylated 4E-BP1 sequesters eIF4E and suppresses translation of the structured-5'UTR HIF1A mRNA; mTORC1 phosphorylation relieves this</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">44; 457; 572</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="MITOCHONDRIAL_BIOGENESIS" id="translational_regulation:0ffe0edfadb7483a">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">MITOCHONDRIAL_BIOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BPs restrain translation of nuclear-encoded mitochondrial mRNAs including TFAM and mitochondrial ribosomal proteins; mTORC1 phosphorylation relieves this</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">421</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="MYC" id="translational_regulation:2b0d6eb9cfd7ea43">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">MYC</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Hypophosphorylated 4E-BP1 binds eIF4E and restrains cap-dependent translation of MYC mRNA, whose structured 5′ untranslated region makes it highly dependent on eIF4F; mTORC1 phosphorylation of 4E-BP1 releases eIF4E and raises c-Myc protein.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">44; 457; 572</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="TFAM" id="translational_regulation:c41de83edb914ed8">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">TFAM</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BP restrains translation of nuclear-encoded mitochondrial mRNAs including TFAM and respiratory chain components; mTORC1-dependent 4E-BP phosphorylation relieves this.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">420; 421</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The primary evidence is translational profiling showing that mTOR inhibition lowers synthesis of TFAM and other nucleus-encoded mitochondrial proteins in wild-type cells but not in cells lacking 4E-BP1 and 4E-BP2.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="VEGFA" id="translational_regulation:090e8796aecd2b40">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">VEGFA</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Hypophosphorylated 4E-BP1 sequesters eIF4E and suppresses translation of VEGFA mRNA, which has a long structured 5′ untranslated region; mTORC1 phosphorylation of 4E-BP1 relieves this, adding a translational arm to HIF-1alpha-dependent VEGFA transcription.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">44; 145; 457; 572</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="PHENO_PHOTIC_ENTRAINMENT" id="other:e84938e9e27551af">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_PHOTIC_ENTRAINMENT</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BP1 restrains entrainment: Eif4ebp1-null mice show accelerated re-entrainment to a shifted light schedule and larger photic phase shifts, mirroring their elevated VIP levels</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">81</data>
  <data key="d17">Eif4ebp1-null mice</data>
  <data key="d18">circadian</data>
  <data key="d19">Accelerated re-entrainment and larger light-induced phase shifts in 4E-BP1-null mice come from a single genetic study and have not been independently replicated.</data>
  <data key="d20">circadian</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP1" target="VIP" id="translational_regulation:dacb60052317961a">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP1</data>
  <data key="d10">VIP</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BP1 represses translation of Vip mRNA in SCN neurons; 4E-BP1 loss raises VIP levels, accelerating re-entrainment and increasing network synchrony.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">81</data>
  <data key="d17">mouse SCN, Eif4ebp1-null</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">PMID 23972597 (Cao R et al., Neuron 2013;79(4):712-724). Repression of VIP translation is supported by the Eif4ebp1-null phenotype in that paper.</data>
  <data key="d20">circadian</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP2" target="EIF4E" id="sequestration:b47f9ad35b15afeb">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP2</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4E</data>
  <data key="d11">sequestration</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BP2, the predominant brain 4E-BP, restrains eIF4F formation; its loss enhances LTP, impairs memory and produces autism-like behaviour via excess eIF4E-dependent translation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">26; 195</data>
  <data key="d17">hippocampal neurons</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP2" target="LTP_LATE_PHASE" id="translational_regulation:21e1cb9f8414f1d9">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP2</data>
  <data key="d10">LTP_LATE_PHASE</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">4E-BP2 represses cap-dependent translation at the synapse: in its absence a single train that normally yields only early-phase potentiation induces late-phase LTP, so the induction threshold falls, yet late-phase LTP elicited by repeated high-frequency trains is impaired and hippocampus-dependent memory is deficient. Both excessive and deficient translational signalling are deleterious in brain.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">26</data>
  <data key="d17">forebrain neuron</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The lowered threshold for late-phase LTP in 4E-BP2-null hippocampus coexists with impaired late-phase LTP after strong stimulation and with deficient hippocampus-dependent memory.</data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="EIF4EBP2" target="PHENO_AUTISM_LIKE_BEHAVIOUR" id="translational_regulation:4535af298af06533">
  <data key="d9">EIF4EBP2</data>
  <data key="d10">PHENO_AUTISM_LIKE_BEHAVIOUR</data>
  <data key="d11">translational_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Loss of the translational repressor 4E-BP2 produces autism-like behaviour with an increased excitation-to-inhibition ratio, the upper arm of the neuronal requirement for calibrated translational signalling; the accompanying synaptic phenotype is a lowered threshold for late-phase LTP rather than uniformly enhanced potentiation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">195</data>
  <data key="d17">CNS neuron</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">tissue_physiology_disease</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LARP1" target="EIF4E" id="competitive_inhibition:ae621947bd89b16b">
  <data key="d9">LARP1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4E</data>
  <data key="d11">competitive_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">The C-terminal DM15 region of LARP1 binds the m7G cap and the adjacent 5'TOP pyrimidine tract at the same time. This occludes the cap from eIF4E and excludes eIF4F from TOP mRNAs when mTORC1 is inactive.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">171; 238; 323; 469; 604</data>
  <data key="d17">mTORC1 inactive / nutrient withdrawal</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LARP1" target="EIF4F" id="competitive_inhibition:9ef716fe03176b72">
  <data key="d9">LARP1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4F</data>
  <data key="d11">competitive_inhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Cap occupancy by LARP1 sterically excludes eIF4E/eIF4F from TOP mRNAs.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">171; 238; 323</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LARP1" target="PABPC1" id="binding:ef8fdb679963f21a">
  <data key="d9">LARP1</data>
  <data key="d10">PABPC1</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">LARP1 associates with PABP and poly(A) tails, linking 3' and 5' ends of TOP mRNAs and contributing to their stability and mTORC1-responsive translation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">171; 598</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LARP1" target="RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS" id="other:bd4713eb9b1b152b">
  <data key="d9">LARP1</data>
  <data key="d10">RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">LARP1 represses TOP mRNAs, which encode the ribosomal proteins and several elongation factors, and so indirectly limits the protein arm of ribosome biogenesis. Relief of this repression on mTORC1 activation lets ribosomal-protein supply scale with the Pol I rRNA and Pol III tRNA and 5S arms.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">171; 238; 255; 341; 401; 458</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LARP1" target="TOP_MRNA" id="binding:5be97630e077f934">
  <data key="d9">LARP1</data>
  <data key="d10">TOP_MRNA</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">When mTORC1 is inactive, the LARP1 DM15 domain binds the m7G cap and adjacent 5'TOP motif, repressing translation of ribosomal-protein and elongation-factor mRNAs.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">171; 238; 323</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="LARP1" target="TOP_MRNA" id="stabilisation:05a723c9d0c4ad8b">
  <data key="d9">LARP1</data>
  <data key="d10">TOP_MRNA</data>
  <data key="d11">stabilisation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Independently of translational repression, LARP1 (with 40S subunits) protects TOP mRNAs from decay; LARP1 depletion lowers TOP mRNA abundance.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">9; 191</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Dual repressor/stabiliser role of LARP1.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MAF1" target="BRF1" id="binding:b75b478d998529aa">
  <data key="d9">MAF1</data>
  <data key="d10">BRF1</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">MAF1 binds the TFIIIB subunit BRF1 and inhibits de novo assembly of TFIIIB-DNA complexes on Pol III promoters, which impairs Pol III recruitment.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">137; 197; 410; 504</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Direct Maf1-Brf1 co-immunoprecipitation was shown in yeast. In mammalian cells, MAF1 associates with TFIIIB and Pol III and represses transcription through BRF1 and BRF2.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MAF1" target="POLR1" id="other:d7ade81ca3916491">
  <data key="d9">MAF1</data>
  <data key="d10">POLR1</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Mammalian MAF1 also represses Pol I transcription, at least partly indirectly, by repressing transcription of TBP, the TATA-binding protein that Pol I initiation complexes require. No direct contact between MAF1 and Pol I has been shown.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">279</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="MAF1" target="POLR3" id="binding:1418347de4a61cc5">
  <data key="d9">MAF1</data>
  <data key="d10">POLR3</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">Dephosphorylated MAF1 binds the Pol III clamp, rearranging the enzyme to prevent TFIIIB-dependent recruitment and closed-complex formation on tRNA and 5S promoters.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">409; 410; 623; 647</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PPP2CA" target="RPS6KB1" id="dephosphorylation:7d6c08e214df2a43">
  <data key="d9">PPP2CA</data>
  <data key="d10">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d11">dephosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">PP2A associates with S6K1 and dephosphorylates it when mTORC1 is inhibited by rapamycin; mTORC1 activity restrains PP2A action on S6K1.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr389</data>
  <data key="d16">464</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Identity of the physiological S6K1 phosphatase remains debated (PP2A vs PHLPP).</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="PPP2CA" target="RRN3" id="dephosphorylation:2c8ce22569c882dd">
  <data key="d9">PPP2CA</data>
  <data key="d10">RRN3</data>
  <data key="d11">dephosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">PP2A dephosphorylates the activating Ser44 site of TIF-IA/RRN3, reducing Pol I transcription; phosphatase inhibition attenuates rapamycin-induced Ser44 dephosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser44</data>
  <data key="d16">400</data>
  <data key="d17">mTOR inhibition by rapamycin</data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">PP2A treatment inactivates TIF-IA in vitro, and calyculin A or okadaic acid prevents rapamycin-induced Ser44 dephosphorylation in cells. Because these inhibitors also act on PP1, PP2A is the most likely cellular phosphatase but not a proven one, and no phosphatase has been identified for Ser199.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="IRS1" id="phosphorylation:4eec79a2a322ec09">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">IRS1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates IRS-1 on inhibitory serines, uncoupling it from INSR/PI3K and promoting its degradation — blunting PI3K–AKT output (negative feedback).</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser1101; Ser307; Ser636; Ser639</data>
  <data key="d16">222; 547; 608; 616</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Sites are human IRS-1 numbering: Ser307 corresponds to mouse Ser302 and Ser636/Ser639 to rat Ser632/Ser635. Human Ser312 (mouse Ser307) is phosphorylated by JNK and IKK rather than by S6K1. This feedback explains the rebound AKT activation seen on acute mTORC1 inhibition [500].</data>
  <data key="d20">growth_factor_input</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="IRS1" id="phosphorylation:95d8f70718953e77">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">IRS1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates IRS-1 on inhibitory serines, promoting its degradation and blunting PI3K-AKT output from the insulin receptor.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser1101; Ser307; Ser636; Ser639</data>
  <data key="d16">222; 608; 616</data>
  <data key="d17">chronic insulin stimulation, obesity</data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Sites are human IRS-1 numbering; the primary reports use the mouse and rat equivalents Ser302 and Ser632/Ser635. Underlies mTORC1-associated insulin resistance and the paradoxical protection from steatosis seen after liver Tsc1 deletion.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="DEPTOR" id="phosphorylation:185b05a5c6d7d46e">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">DEPTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1, together with RSK1, was reported to phosphorylate the DEPTOR betaTrCP degron (Ser286, Ser287, Ser291) after growth-factor stimulation, promoting SCF-betaTrCP-dependent DEPTOR degradation. Another laboratory found that S6K inhibition or knockdown does not change DEPTOR turnover and attributed degron phosphorylation to mTOR and CK1alpha.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">701</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Opposing evidence: Gao et al. 2011, Mol Cell 44:290-303 (PMID 22017875).</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="MAPKAP1" id="phosphorylation:74a633b5c02df633">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">MAPKAP1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates mSin1 at Thr86 and Thr398, impairing mTORC2 complex integrity and suppressing downstream AKT signalling.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr398; Thr86</data>
  <data key="d16">359</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Yang et al. 2015 (Cell Rep 12:937-943; PMID 26235620) reported that AKT-mediated phosphorylation of mSin1 Thr86 enhances mTORC2 activity, the opposite sign at the same residue.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="MAPKAP1" id="phosphorylation:74f68052cd36a390">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">MAPKAP1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1-dependent mSin1 Thr86/Thr398 phosphorylation was reported to disrupt mTORC2 and reduce AKT Ser473 phosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr398; Thr86</data>
  <data key="d16">359</data>
  <data key="d17">reported in tumour cell lines</data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Liu et al. reported negative regulation involving Thr86/Thr398; Yang et al. reported positive AKT-dependent regulation specifically at Thr86.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc2_regulation_and_outputs</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="MTOR" id="phosphorylation:1d9ee5f399123e14">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">MTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates mTOR at Ser2448 in the negative regulatory domain; the site is a convenient activity marker but has no demonstrated effect on catalysis.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser2448</data>
  <data key="d16">106; 237</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Established as a phosphorylation event; its functional consequence for mTOR activity is not established.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="RICTOR" id="phosphorylation:265ff1996d671131">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">RICTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">mTORC1-activated S6K1 directly phosphorylates Rictor at Thr1135, creating a 14-3-3 binding site without altering mTORC2 assembly or in vitro kinase activity. Two studies found that a T1135A mutant raises AKT Ser473 phosphorylation, indicating feedback inhibition, whereas two others saw no major change in mTORC2 activity or AKT phosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d14">contested</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr1135</data>
  <data key="d16">53; 142; 285; 607</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">feedback</data>
  <data key="d19">Boulbès et al. 2010 [53] detected Thr1135 phosphorylation in mTORC2-deficient cells, suggesting that it may also regulate functions of Rictor outside mTORC2.</data>
  <data key="d20">core_complexes</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="RICTOR" id="phosphorylation:661c2b076554d92c">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">RICTOR</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
