<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="CELL_GROWTH" id="physiological_output:fe0729fe6484c218">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">CELL_GROWTH</data>
  <data key="d11">physiological_output</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 deletion yields small mice and cells; S6Ks control cell size (growth) whereas 4E-BPs control proliferation downstream of mTORC1.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">148; 561</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="EEF2K" id="phosphorylation:40d0ae9c2419347e">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">EEF2K</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates eEF2 kinase at Ser366, reducing its activity at physiological Ca2+ and thereby lowering eEF2 Thr56 phosphorylation and accelerating elongation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser366</data>
  <data key="d16">636</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="EIF4B" id="phosphorylation:0aed758abdcd9ab8">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">EIF4B</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates eIF4B at Ser422 in a rapamycin-sensitive manner, enhancing eIF4B recruitment to the preinitiation complex and eIF4A helicase stimulation on structured 5'UTRs.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser422</data>
  <data key="d16">236; 487; 548</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="NCBP1" id="phosphorylation:934291941e6a1473">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">NCBP1</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates CBP80, the large subunit of the nuclear cap-binding complex, at residues that are also phosphorylated in cells in a growth-factor-dependent, rapamycin-sensitive manner, and S6K1 is found together with SKAR on CBP80-bound spliced mRNAs.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">370; 651</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The phosphorylated residues of CBP80 have not been mapped, and the consequence for cap binding and splicing remains undetermined.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="PDCD4" id="phosphorylation:e97d4b48ca6544ff">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">PDCD4</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates PDCD4 at Ser67, creating a phosphodegron for betaTrCP-mediated ubiquitination and proteasomal degradation, relieving eIF4A inhibition.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser67</data>
  <data key="d16">147</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="PDK1" id="binding:ac46a78928e8fa2d">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">PDK1</data>
  <data key="d11">binding</data>
  <data key="d12">required_for</data>
  <data key="d13">Phosphorylation of the S6K1 hydrophobic motif at Thr389 by mTORC1 creates a docking site for the PIF pocket of PDK1, which then phosphorylates the S6K1 activation loop at Thr229. The mTORC1 and PDK1 inputs to S6K1 are therefore sequential rather than independent.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Thr389</data>
  <data key="d16">46; 179; 378; 481</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Thr389 lies in the hydrophobic motif of S6K1, the docking partner; PDK1 itself is not phosphorylated in this interaction.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS" id="transcriptional_regulation:886092f64a80ce81">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">RIBOSOME_BIOGENESIS</data>
  <data key="d11">transcriptional_regulation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K activity controls a ribosome-biogenesis transcriptional programme (rRNA processing factors, Pol I/III cofactors) in addition to rDNA transcription.</data>
  <data key="d14">emerging</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">97; 255; 458</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">The mammalian evidence comes mainly from liver and cells lacking both S6K1 and S6K2, in which the feeding-induced ribosome-biogenesis transcriptional programme fails. The requirement for S6K1 in UBF-dependent rDNA transcription is a separate and better-supported arm.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="RPS6" id="phosphorylation:8bcca41aa70ae533">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">RPS6</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">Active S6K1 phosphorylates 40S ribosomal protein S6 in ordered fashion (Ser236 first); phosphorylation influences cell size and glucose homeostasis.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser235; Ser236; Ser240; Ser244</data>
  <data key="d16">408; 512</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Ser247 is phosphorylated by CK1 after priming.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="RPS6KB1" id="autoinhibition:67dd19c065fc6ccf">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d11">autoinhibition</data>
  <data key="d12">inhibits</data>
  <data key="d13">The C-terminal autoinhibitory pseudosubstrate domain restrains S6K1; proline-directed phosphorylation (Ser411/Thr421/Ser424) relieves it, allowing Thr389/Thr229 phosphorylation.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">378</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">-1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="SKAR" id="phosphorylation:8acbb8d4f63aa4be">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">SKAR</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">modulates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1, but not S6K2, binds SKAR and phosphorylates it at Ser383 and Ser385 in response to insulin and other mitogens, in a rapamycin-sensitive manner. Depletion of SKAR reduces cell size, but the functional consequence of these two phosphorylations has not been determined.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser383; Ser385</data>
  <data key="d16">369; 493</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19"></data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">0</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="UBTF" id="other:450dfb4b54972cef">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">UBTF</data>
  <data key="d11">other</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 activity is required for mTOR-dependent phosphorylation of the UBF carboxy-terminal activation domain, which enhances UBF binding to SL1 and Pol I transcription of rDNA. Rapamycin causes rapid dephosphorylation of this domain, and a constitutively active, rapamycin-resistant S6K1 reverses it. Direct phosphorylation of UBF by S6K1 has not been shown, so the relationship is S6K1-dependent rather than a proven kinase-substrate interaction.</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15"></data>
  <data key="d16">214</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Rapamycin-sensitive; whether S6K1 phosphorylates UBF directly or via an intermediate kinase is not fully resolved.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_translation_ribosome</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
<edge source="RPS6KB1" target="CAD" id="phosphorylation:b191d8c5a84639f9">
  <data key="d9">RPS6KB1</data>
  <data key="d10">CAD</data>
  <data key="d11">phosphorylation</data>
  <data key="d12">activates</data>
  <data key="d13">S6K1 phosphorylates CAD at Ser1859, promoting its oligomerisation and raising flux through the first three steps of de novo pyrimidine synthesis</data>
  <data key="d14">established</data>
  <data key="d15">Ser1859</data>
  <data key="d16">35; 497</data>
  <data key="d17"></data>
  <data key="d18">downstream_of_mTORC1</data>
  <data key="d19">Demonstrated independently by two groups in the same year, one by unbiased phosphoproteomics.</data>
  <data key="d20">mtorc1_output_autophagy_metabolism_feedback</data>
  <data key="d21">1</data>
</edge>
